// Spoken answers (60-90 s) for python_sre guide
(window.SPOKEN = window.SPOKEN || {})['python_sre'] = {
  1: {
    a: `Immutable objects such as ints, strings, tuples and frozensets cannot change after creation, while mutable ones such as lists, dicts and sets can be modified in place. Variables are references, so assigning one list to another name gives two names for the same object, and a change through one is visible through the other, which surprises people in scripts that pass configuration around. The classic bug is a mutable default argument: the default value is evaluated once when the function is defined, so a function with an empty list default shares that same list across calls, and results accumulate between invocations. In an automation script that might leak hosts from one run into the next. The fix is to use None as the default and create a new list inside the function. To avoid aliasing, copy with the copy method, slicing, or copy.deepcopy for nested structures. I also prefer immutable tuples or frozen dataclasses for configuration that must not change.`,
    f: ["How do you copy a nested dictionary safely?","Why are dictionary keys required to be hashable?"]
  },
  2: {
    a: `A comprehension builds a collection in one expression: square brackets make a list, curly braces with key and value make a dictionary, and curly braces with a single expression make a set. They are concise and usually faster than an equivalent loop with append. A generator expression uses parentheses and produces items lazily, one at a time, without building the whole collection in memory. For automation this matters with large inputs: summing the sizes of a million log entries with a generator expression uses constant memory, while a list comprehension would allocate the whole list. I use comprehensions when the result is needed as a collection and the logic is simple, for example filtering running instances or building a lookup dictionary by instance ID. When a comprehension needs nested conditions or side effects, a plain loop is clearer. Generators also compose with functions like any, all, sum and join, short-circuiting where possible.`,
    f: ["When does a generator expression save memory?","Why avoid side effects inside comprehensions?"]
  },
  3: {
    a: `A generator function contains yield and returns an iterator that produces values one at a time, pausing at each yield and resuming where it left off, keeping its local state between calls. This lets you process data as a stream instead of loading everything into memory. In operations work, a generator that reads a multi-gigabyte log line by line, filters errors and yields parsed records lets you chain stages, read, parse, filter and aggregate, each consuming the previous one lazily, similar to a Unix pipeline, with memory use staying flat. Generators are also useful for paginated APIs: a function that yields items page by page hides pagination from the caller. Related features include yield from to delegate to another generator, send to pass values in, and closing with try and finally for cleanup. A generator can be iterated only once, so if you need multiple passes you must recreate it or materialise the results. They make code readable and efficient together.`,
    f: ["Why can a generator be consumed only once?","How does yield from help with pagination?"]
  },
  4: {
    a: `A decorator is a function that takes a function and returns a new function, usually wrapping the original to add behaviour before or after the call. The at-sign syntax is shorthand for reassigning the name to the wrapped version. Inside the wrapper you call the original with its arguments and return the result, and you apply functools.wraps so the wrapped function keeps its name and docstring, which matters for logging and debugging. Decorators can take arguments by adding another outer function. Useful applications in operations scripts include retrying a flaky API call with backoff, timing how long a function takes, logging entry and exit, caching results, enforcing permissions, rate limiting, and registering functions as commands or handlers, as frameworks like click and FastAPI do. Class-based decorators are possible too. A caution is that heavy stacking of decorators can obscure control flow and stack traces, so I keep them focused and well tested.`,
    f: ["Why use functools.wraps?","How do you write a decorator that takes arguments?"]
  },
  5: {
    a: `A context manager defines setup and cleanup around a block, used with the with statement. Entering calls the enter method and leaving, even through an exception, calls exit, so resources are released reliably. Files, locks, database connections, temporary directories and subprocess handles all support it. In scripts this prevents leaks, such as leaving a file open or a lock held when an error occurs. You can write your own either as a class with enter and exit methods, or more simply with the contextlib.contextmanager decorator on a generator that yields once, with try and finally around the yield for cleanup. Practical examples: a context manager that takes a lock file, a timer that logs elapsed time, a temporary change of working directory or environment variable, or one that opens an SSH session and always closes it. contextlib also offers suppress for expected exceptions and ExitStack to manage a dynamic number of contexts.`,
    f: ["How does contextlib.contextmanager work?","When would you use ExitStack?"]
  },
  6: {
    a: `The aim is to fail clearly, clean up and never hide real problems. I catch specific exceptions I can handle, such as a connection timeout or a missing file, and let unexpected ones propagate, since a bare except or catching Exception silently turns bugs into mysterious behaviour. When I catch, I either recover meaningfully, retry, or add context and re-raise, using raise from to keep the original cause. Use finally or context managers for cleanup, and the else clause for code that should run only when no exception happened. For scripts, the top level catches known error types, logs them with enough detail, including the traceback at debug level, and exits with a nonzero status so cron, CI and orchestrators notice. Custom exception classes express domain errors like a quota exceeded or a failed validation. I avoid using exceptions for normal control flow in hot paths, and I never swallow KeyboardInterrupt or SystemExit by accident.`,
    f: ["Why avoid a bare except?","What does raise from do?"]
  },
  7: {
    a: `Type hints annotate parameters, returns and variables with expected types. Python does not enforce them at run time, but tools such as mypy and editors check them, catching mistakes like passing a string where a list is expected, and they document intent for the next reader. Dataclasses reduce boilerplate for classes that mainly hold data, generating init, repr and equality, with options for immutability through frozen and for default factories. Pydantic goes further by validating and converting data at run time: you define a model with typed fields, and constructing it from a dictionary or JSON checks types, applies defaults and raises clear errors, which is ideal for configuration files, API responses and environment settings. In operational code that handles messy inputs, this catches problems at the boundary instead of deep inside a script. I use hints everywhere in shared code, dataclasses for internal records and pydantic for external input, and run mypy in CI to keep them accurate.`,
    f: ["Does Python enforce type hints at run time?","When would you choose pydantic over a dataclass?"]
  },
  8: {
    a: `The Global Interpreter Lock is a mutex in the standard CPython interpreter that lets only one thread execute Python bytecode at a time. As a result, threads do not give true parallelism for CPU-bound Python code, since they take turns, but they work well for I/O-bound work, because the lock is released while waiting on the network, disk or a subprocess. Most infrastructure scripts are I/O-bound, calling APIs, running SSH commands or reading files, so a thread pool or asyncio gives real speedups. For CPU-bound work, such as parsing large datasets or hashing many files, use multiprocessing or a process pool, which uses separate interpreters, or move hot loops to native extensions, as libraries like numpy and cryptography do. Recent Python versions are making the lock optional through experimental free-threaded builds, but code should not depend on that yet. So the rule is threads or asyncio for waiting, processes for computing.`,
    f: ["Why do threads help for API calls but not for hashing?","What is a free-threaded build?"]
  },
  9: {
    a: `Python resolves names using the LEGB rule: local, enclosing, global, then built-in. Assigning to a name inside a function makes it local unless declared global or nonlocal, which is why modifying a module-level counter inside a function without the global keyword raises an error. A closure is a function that remembers variables from its enclosing scope even after that scope has finished, which is how decorators and factory functions work. A frequent pitfall is late binding: closures capture the variable, not its value at creation time, so functions created in a loop all see the final value of the loop variable. For example a list of callbacks built in a loop each using the loop variable will all use the last one. The fix is to bind the value with a default argument, use functools.partial, or create the function in a helper. Related pitfalls include shadowing built-in names like list or id, and unintentionally modifying global state, which makes scripts hard to test.`,
    f: ["How do you fix late binding in a list of callbacks?","What does nonlocal do?"]
  },
  10: {
    a: `In Python 3 a str is a sequence of Unicode characters, while bytes is a sequence of raw bytes, and they are not interchangeable. Converting uses encode and decode with an encoding, usually UTF-8. Problems arise at boundaries: files opened in text mode use a default encoding that depends on the environment, so scripts behave differently in a container with a C locale, and I therefore pass encoding explicitly. Logs may contain invalid byte sequences, so decoding with the errors argument set to replace or backslashreplace avoids crashes, while binary files are read in binary mode. Subprocess output is bytes unless text mode or an encoding is requested. Network protocols and hashing functions need bytes, so encode before hashing. F-strings are the preferred way to format text, with format specifiers for widths and precision, and repr or the r conversion for debugging. For data handling I decode at the input boundary, work in str internally and encode at the output boundary.`,
    f: ["How do you read a log file with invalid UTF-8?","Why must text be encoded before hashing?"]
  },
  11: {
    a: `A module is a Python file and a package is a directory of modules, traditionally with an init file, imported by name through the module search path, which includes the script directory, installed site-packages and the PYTHONPATH. Importing runs the module's top-level code once and caches it. Relative imports inside a package use dots. The name dunder variable equals main when a file is run directly and the module name when imported, so the guard if name equals main protects code that should run only as a script, allowing the same file to be imported by tests without side effects. I structure projects with a package directory, thin entry points, a pyproject file and tests alongside, and install the project in editable mode for development so imports work the same as in production. Pitfalls are circular imports, which I fix by restructuring or importing inside functions, naming a file like a standard library module such as json.py, which shadows it, and modifying sys.path hacks.`,
    f: ["Why does naming a file json.py break things?","How do you avoid circular imports?"]
  },
  12: {
    a: `The key is to avoid loading it all into memory. Iterating over a file object reads it line by line with buffering, so a for loop over the file handles gigabytes with constant memory. I combine that with generators to filter and transform lazily, compile regular expressions once outside the loop, and keep per-line work cheap. For counting or aggregating, I use collections.Counter or defaultdict and update them as I stream. For compressed files, gzip.open in text mode streams the same way. When a file is too large for one core, I split it into byte ranges aligned to line boundaries and process chunks in a process pool, then merge results. To follow a live log, I read, then seek and poll like tail, and handle rotation by detecting inode changes. For extremely large analytic work I consider a proper tool, such as grep, awk, ripgrep or loading into DuckDB or a log platform, since a Python loop is rarely the fastest option for a simple search.`,
    f: ["How do you tail a file that is rotated?","When is awk or ripgrep better than Python?"]
  },
  13: {
    a: `For JSON the standard json module handles loads and dumps, and I pass indent and sort keys for readable, stable output that produces clean diffs in Git. For YAML I use PyYAML or ruamel.yaml, and the critical rule is to call safe_load, never load without a safe loader, because the full loader can construct arbitrary Python objects and execute code from untrusted input. Ruamel preserves comments and ordering, useful when editing files a human maintains. TOML is read with the standard tomllib module from Python 3.11, which is read-only, with a separate library such as tomli-w for writing. Whatever the format, I validate the structure after loading, with pydantic or jsonschema, so a missing key fails with a clear message. When writing configuration files that other processes read, I write to a temporary file and atomically rename, to avoid readers seeing a partial file. I also open files with an explicit encoding and avoid putting secrets into files that may be committed.`,
    f: ["Why is yaml.load dangerous?","Why write to a temp file then rename?"]
  },
  14: {
    a: `The re module compiles patterns, and I compile once and reuse. For structured parsing I use named groups, so the result is a dictionary of fields such as client IP, timestamp, method, path and status, which is much clearer than numeric group indexes. Use search to find a pattern anywhere, match to anchor at the start, fullmatch for the entire string and finditer for all occurrences, with raw strings to avoid backslash confusion. Pitfalls: catastrophic backtracking with nested quantifiers on adversarial or long inputs, which can freeze a script, so keep patterns specific and avoid overlapping wildcards; greedy matching that spans more than intended, fixed with non-greedy quantifiers or negated character classes; fragile regexes on formats with proper parsers, where JSON, XML or well-defined formats should use a real parser; and unhandled non-matching lines, where match returns None and attribute access fails. For common formats like Apache logs, a maintained parser or splitting on known delimiters can be simpler and faster. I test patterns with sample lines, including bad ones.`,
    f: ["What is catastrophic backtracking?","When is a regex the wrong tool?"]
  },
  15: {
    a: `Pathlib models paths as objects: the slash operator joins paths, and methods such as exists, is_file, mkdir with parents and exist_ok, glob and rglob, read_text, write_text, stat and unlink handle common operations in a portable, readable way, replacing a mix of os.path calls. Shutil adds higher-level operations: copy and copy2, copytree, move, rmtree, disk_usage and which. For safe writes, I avoid truncating the destination first. The atomic pattern is to write the new content to a temporary file in the same directory, flush and fsync it, then use os.replace to rename over the destination, which is atomic on the same filesystem, so readers see either the old or the new file, never a partial one. I set permissions explicitly, especially for files containing secrets, with a restrictive mode from creation using os.open flags or tempfile. For deletion, I check paths carefully before rmtree, to avoid removing the wrong directory through an empty variable.`,
    f: ["Why must the temp file be in the same directory?","How do you guard against a dangerous rmtree?"]
  },
  16: {
    a: `I use subprocess.run with a list of arguments and without shell mode, which avoids shell injection and quoting bugs, because each argument is passed directly. I set check true so a nonzero exit raises CalledProcessError, capture_output and text to collect decoded output, a timeout so a hung command cannot block the script forever, and an explicit env and cwd when needed. If a shell feature is truly required, I use shlex.quote on any untrusted value, but usually I do the piping in Python. For long-running commands I stream output with Popen and iterate the stdout pipe, taking care of deadlocks when both stdout and stderr fill their buffers, avoided by merging stderr into stdout or using communicate. I log the command and exit code, but never secrets passed as arguments, since they appear in process listings; secrets go through environment variables or stdin. I handle FileNotFoundError when the binary is missing, and terminate the process tree on timeout where required. Whenever a Python library exists, it is better than shelling out.`,
    f: ["Why is shell=True dangerous?","How do you avoid a deadlock reading stdout and stderr?"]
  },
  17: {
    a: `The standard library covers the common formats: the tarfile module reads and writes tar archives with compression modes for gzip, bz2 and xz, the zipfile module handles zip files, and gzip, bz2 and lzma handle single compressed streams. Shutil.make_archive and unpack_archive provide quick helpers. Security is the main concern when extracting archives from untrusted sources: a malicious tar or zip can contain paths with parent directory references or absolute paths, or symlinks, that write outside the target directory, known as path traversal or zip slip. Newer Python versions add an extraction filter parameter to tarfile, recommended as data, which rejects dangerous members, and for older versions I validate each member's resolved path against the target directory before extracting. Also limit the total size and number of files to resist decompression bombs. For backups I stream data into the archive rather than building it in memory, and add checksums so that restore can verify integrity. I record permissions and ownership only when the use case needs them.`,
    f: ["What is a zip slip attack?","What does the tarfile data filter do?"]
  },
  18: {
    a: `I define a clear precedence and a single place that resolves it. A common order from lowest to highest is built-in defaults, a configuration file, environment variables, then command-line flags, so operators can override anything at run time without editing files. Environment variables follow the twelve-factor style, with a prefix for the tool, for example MYTOOL_REGION. I model the final configuration as a typed object, using pydantic settings or a dataclass, which validates types, applies defaults and gives clear errors for missing required values, and the rest of the code receives that object instead of reading os.environ everywhere, which keeps it testable. Secrets are read from environment variables, files mounted by the orchestrator or a secrets manager, never defaulted or logged, and printed configuration is redacted. I document each option, support a dry-run or print-config flag to show the effective values, and keep configuration files in a format such as YAML or TOML with safe loading.`,
    f: ["Why have one place that resolves configuration?","How do you redact secrets in logs?"]
  },
  19: {
    a: `Predictable temporary file names in a shared directory like tmp are a security risk, since another user could create a symlink with that name and trick the script into overwriting something else, known as a symlink attack. The tempfile module avoids this: NamedTemporaryFile, TemporaryDirectory and mkstemp create uniquely named files with restrictive permissions, opened exclusively so they cannot be pre-created by someone else. TemporaryDirectory as a context manager removes the directory and its contents when the block exits, even on errors, which keeps scripts clean. When a file must be visible to another process, I use delete false and clean up explicitly, or create the temp file next to the destination for atomic replacement. For sensitive data, I avoid writing to disk at all, or use a directory on a memory-backed filesystem, and remove it securely when done. I never build paths by string concatenation with user input, instead resolving and checking that the result stays within an expected directory.`,
    f: ["What is a symlink attack on a temp file?","When would you use mkstemp?"]
  },
  20: {
    a: `The hashlib module provides algorithms such as SHA-256, and for files I read in chunks and update the hash incrementally, so memory use is constant even for very large files. Chunk reads of one megabyte or so are typical, and Python 3.11 adds hashlib.file_digest. For integrity verification of downloads I compare against a published checksum using hmac.compare_digest to avoid timing leaks when it matters. To compare directories, I walk both trees, build a mapping of relative path to hash, size and mode, and diff the dictionaries to find added, removed and changed files, or use filecmp.dircmp for a quick comparison. For change detection at scale, I first compare size and modification time, and compute hashes only when needed. Note that MD5 and SHA-1 are broken for security purposes, fine only for non-security checks such as cache keys, so I default to SHA-256. For configuration drift tasks, I normalise things like line endings and ordering before comparing, to avoid false positives.`,
    f: ["Why read the file in chunks?","Why not use MD5 for integrity of downloads?"]
  },
  21: {
    a: `Argparse is in the standard library, so it needs no dependency, which suits small scripts and environments where installing packages is hard. It supports subcommands, types, choices and help text but is verbose. Click is a widely used third-party library built on decorators, giving concise definitions, nested command groups, prompts, colours, progress bars, file handling and good testing support through its runner. Typer builds on click and uses Python type hints to define arguments and options, which makes the code very compact and produces help and shell completion automatically. For a quick script I use argparse; for a tool that will grow, with subcommands and users, I use click or typer. Whatever I choose, good practice is meaningful help text, sensible defaults, a version flag, a dry-run option for changes, nonzero exit codes on failure, environment variable fallbacks, and keeping the logic in importable functions so it can be tested without invoking the command line.`,
    f: ["What does typer add over click?","How do you test a click command?"]
  },
  22: {
    a: `Exit codes are how scripts tell cron, CI systems, shells and orchestrators whether they succeeded: zero means success and any nonzero means failure, and conventions distinguish kinds of failure, for example one for general errors and two for usage errors. In Python, sys.exit with a number, or raising SystemExit, sets it, and an uncaught exception exits with one. If a script swallows errors and exits zero, automation continues as if nothing happened, which is a common and costly bug, so a top-level handler should map failures to nonzero codes. Signals notify a process asynchronously: SIGTERM requests a graceful stop, as sent by Kubernetes and systemd, SIGINT comes from Ctrl-C, and SIGKILL cannot be caught. The signal module lets a script register handlers to finish current work, flush output, release locks and exit cleanly within the grace period. I also make sure child processes are terminated, and that handlers do minimal work, usually setting a flag checked by the main loop.`,
    f: ["Why can SIGKILL not be handled?","What should a SIGTERM handler do?"]
  },
  23: {
    a: `I use the standard logging module rather than print. Each module gets a logger named with name dunder, so the hierarchy reflects the package, and libraries only create loggers and never configure handlers, leaving that to the application. The application configures logging once at startup, with level, format and handlers, through basicConfig, dictConfig or a file. A good format includes timestamp, level, logger name and message, and for services I use structured JSON logs so a central system can index fields, including request or job IDs through logging adapters or context variables. I log to standard error or standard output for containers and let the platform collect them, use rotating handlers only for traditional file logging, and choose levels carefully: debug for detail, info for milestones, warning for recoverable issues, error for failures. I pass arguments lazily with percent formatting rather than building strings, log exceptions with exc_info or the exception method, and never log secrets or personal data.`,
    f: ["Why should libraries not configure logging?","How do you add a request ID to every log line?"]
  },
  24: {
    a: `Tools that compose well follow Unix conventions. Read from standard input when no file argument is given, using sys.stdin iterated line by line so it streams, or fileinput to accept files or stdin uniformly. Write results to standard output and keep diagnostics, progress and errors on standard error, so piping the output into another command is not polluted. Provide machine-friendly output, such as one record per line or JSON lines, with an option for a human-readable table, and handle a closed pipe gracefully: when the downstream command like head exits early, writing raises BrokenPipeError, which should be caught or the SIGPIPE default restored to exit quietly. Respect exit codes, and avoid interactive prompts when input is not a terminal, detected with isatty. Flush output when streaming so consumers see data promptly, and process input lazily rather than reading everything. Support a dash for stdin in file arguments. These details make a script feel like a native tool and make it easy to compose with grep, jq and xargs.`,
    f: ["How do you avoid a BrokenPipeError traceback with head?","Why send progress to stderr?"]
  },
  25: {
    a: `A re-runnable script describes desired state and checks current state before acting. Instead of blindly creating a resource, it looks it up and creates it only if missing, or updates it if different, as in ensure-style functions. I use stable identifiers and names or tags, so a second run finds what the first created. For destructive or one-time actions, I record progress, using a state file or an external marker, so a script that failed halfway can resume without repeating completed steps, and I make each step independently idempotent. Deletions are guarded by checks, confirmations and a dry-run mode that prints intended changes, which I run first. I handle partial failures with cleanup in finally blocks, use locks to prevent concurrent runs, and make API calls with idempotency tokens where supported. Logging says whether each action was skipped or performed. Finally, I test by running twice and asserting that the second run makes no changes, the same principle used with configuration management tools.`,
    f: ["How do idempotency tokens protect against duplicate creates?","What goes in a dry-run output?"]
  },
  26: {
    a: `Retries help with transient failures such as timeouts, connection resets, throttling responses and server errors, but they must be bounded and polite. The standard approach is exponential backoff: wait a base delay, double it after each failure up to a maximum, and add random jitter, so many clients that failed at the same moment do not retry in lockstep and create a thundering herd. I retry only on errors that are likely transient, such as timeouts, connection errors, HTTP 429 and 5xx, and never on client errors like 400, 401 or 404, which will not improve. Operations must be idempotent, or use idempotency keys, before retrying writes. Respect the Retry-After header when present, set an overall deadline and a maximum number of attempts, and log each retry. Libraries such as tenacity or urllib3's Retry and boto3's built-in retry modes implement this well, and are preferable to hand-written loops. For long-running consumers, add a circuit breaker so a failing dependency is not hammered.`,
    f: ["Why add jitter to backoff?","Which HTTP errors should not be retried?"]
  },
  27: {
    a: `Scripts that change production need guard rails. A dry-run flag runs all the discovery and decision logic but replaces each mutating call with a log line describing what would happen, and I make it the default for risky tools, requiring an explicit apply flag. Confirmation prompts show a summary, such as how many resources will be deleted and in which account and region, and require typing a specific word for destructive actions, with a flag to skip the prompt in CI. I print the target context prominently, because running against the wrong account is a classic mistake. Limits help too: a maximum number of items to change per run, with an override, protects against a bug deleting everything. Progress reporting, with counts and an estimated time through a library like tqdm or periodic log lines, helps operators trust long runs, and a final summary lists successes, failures and skipped items. Everything is logged with timestamps for audit, and exit codes reflect partial failure.`,
    f: ["Why make dry-run the default for risky tools?","What is a blast-radius limit?"]
  },
  28: {
    a: `There are several layers. Paramiko is a pure Python SSH library that gives low-level control: connect, run commands, transfer files with SFTP and manage keys. Fabric builds on Paramiko with a friendlier API for running commands and transferring files across many hosts, with connection groups. Netmiko and similar libraries target network devices. Ansible is often the better tool for configuration at scale, and can be invoked through ansible-runner from Python when programmatic control is required. Simpler cases can call the system ssh through subprocess, which reuses the user's config, agent and ControlMaster. Practices I follow: use key-based authentication with an agent, verify host keys instead of auto-adding them, set timeouts, run commands with explicit error checking of exit status, collect stdout and stderr, parallelise with a thread pool for many hosts while limiting concurrency, and avoid putting secrets in command lines. I also decide whether Python is the right place for this logic, since an Ansible playbook may be more maintainable than a custom SSH script.`,
    f: ["Why not auto-add unknown host keys?","When would you use Ansible instead of Paramiko?"]
  },
  29: {
    a: `I use Bash for short glue scripts that mostly chain existing commands, run in a known environment and are only a few dozen lines, since it is the lowest-friction way to invoke programs and pipes. I switch to Python when logic grows: when I need data structures beyond arrays, parsing of JSON, YAML or APIs, error handling beyond set -e, loops with real conditions, concurrency, unit tests, cross-platform behaviour, or the script will be maintained by a team. Bash has well known pitfalls: word splitting and quoting bugs, fragile handling of filenames with spaces, weak error handling, no real data types and limited testing. Python gives libraries, clear exception handling, type hints, packaging and tests, at the cost of needing an interpreter and dependencies, which can be a problem on minimal or locked-down hosts, where the standard library only approach or a single-file zipapp helps. A common rule of thumb is that when a Bash script passes about one hundred lines or needs a dictionary, it is time for Python.`,
    f: ["What are common Bash pitfalls that Python avoids?","How do you ship Python to minimal hosts?"]
  },
  30: {
    a: `I turn the script into a small package with a pyproject file. The code lives in a package directory with a main function, and the pyproject declares the project name, version, dependencies and a scripts entry point mapping a command name to that function, so installing creates an executable on the user's path. Build with the standard build tool to create a wheel and source distribution, and install with pip, preferably pipx for isolated command-line tools, or uv tool install, which keeps dependencies separate from system Python. For internal distribution, publish to a private package index, or install straight from a Git tag. Include a version flag, a README, a changelog and tests, and pin or bound dependencies sensibly. For environments with no network or a fixed interpreter, a zipapp or a bundled executable made with PyInstaller can be copied as one file, although it is larger and platform specific. Releases are built and published by CI from tags, so installations are reproducible.`,
    f: ["Why use pipx for command-line tools?","What does a scripts entry point do?"]
  },
  31: {
    a: `I use a library rather than urllib. Requests is the classic, simple choice, and httpx offers a similar API with HTTP/2 and async support. The important habits are: always set a timeout, because by default requests waits forever and a hung call can freeze a whole automation run; reuse a Session or Client so connections are pooled and headers and authentication are set once; call raise_for_status so HTTP errors become exceptions; and configure retries with backoff for transient failures through a mounted adapter or the transport options. I verify TLS certificates, never disabling verification to silence an error, but pointing to the right CA bundle instead. For JSON I use the json method and check content types, and I handle pagination and rate limits. Secrets go into headers from environment or a secrets manager, never logged, and I log request IDs. I wrap the API in a small client class, which centralises the base URL, authentication, error mapping and makes it easy to mock in tests.`,
    f: ["Why must you always set a timeout?","How do you add retries to a requests session?"]
  },
  32: {
    a: `Most list APIs return results in pages, with a cursor, a next-page token, a page number or a link header, so a client must loop until no more pages. I wrap this in a generator that yields items, hiding pagination from the caller and keeping memory low, which works nicely with comprehension and processing pipelines. For rate limits, I read the response headers or the 429 status, honour Retry-After, and use backoff with jitter. For polite throughput I limit concurrency, use a token bucket or a simple sleep between calls, and cache stable data to reduce calls. When a collection changes during iteration, cursor-based pagination is safer than offset-based, which can skip or duplicate items. Where the API supports filters, fields selection or bulk endpoints, I use them to reduce calls, and where it supports webhooks or change feeds, I prefer them over polling. I log counts and page numbers for debugging, and fail clearly if a page is malformed.`,
    f: ["Why is cursor pagination safer than offset?","How do you implement a token bucket limiter?"]
  },
  33: {
    a: `Boto3 is the AWS SDK for Python. A client maps one-to-one to service API calls and returns dictionaries, and is the recommended low-level interface, while resources offer an object-oriented layer that is in maintenance mode for many services. Many list calls return truncated results, so I use paginators, which iterate across pages automatically, rather than a single call that silently returns only the first page, a common bug. Waiters poll until a resource reaches a state, such as an instance running or a snapshot completed, with configurable delay and attempts, so I avoid hand-written sleep loops. Credentials come from the default chain: environment, shared config, SSO or an instance or workload role, never hard-coded, and I use assume-role sessions for cross-account work. Configure retries and timeouts with botocore Config, handle ClientError by examining the error code, reuse sessions and clients, create one per thread, and use filters server-side to reduce data. For testing, I use moto or stubber to avoid hitting real AWS.`,
    f: ["Why use a paginator instead of a single call?","How do you assume a role across accounts?"]
  },
  34: {
    a: `The official kubernetes client library wraps the API with typed objects. You load configuration with load_kube_config for local use or load_incluster_config inside a pod, create an API object such as CoreV1Api or AppsV1Api, and call methods to list, create, patch and delete resources, or use the watch utility to stream events. It also supports dynamic clients for custom resources. I use it for programmatic tasks that go beyond a shell script: reporting across namespaces and clusters, cleaning up resources by rules, controllers and operators written with frameworks like kopf, or tools that need structured data and error handling. For simple one-off actions, kubectl is quicker and clearer. Practices: use service accounts with least-privilege RBAC when running in-cluster, set timeouts and handle ApiException status codes such as 404 and 409, page large lists with limit and continue tokens, avoid polling in favour of watches, and be careful with resourceVersion when updating, using patch instead of full replace to avoid conflicts.`,
    f: ["How do you authenticate inside a pod?","Why prefer patch to replace?"]
  },
  35: {
    a: `The method depends on the API. Simple services use a static API key or bearer token in a header, loaded from environment or a secrets manager. Many platforms use OAuth 2.0: for machine-to-machine automation the client credentials flow exchanges a client ID and secret for a short-lived access token, which the client caches and refreshes before it expires, typically by tracking the expiry time with a safety margin and refreshing on a 401 response as a fallback. Cloud providers use signed requests or short-lived credentials from roles, handled by their SDKs. In a client class I centralise this: a method that returns a valid token, thread-safe refresh so that concurrent callers do not all refresh at once, and an authenticated session that adds the header. Secrets are never logged or committed, and I scope tokens to minimum permissions. For mutual TLS, a client certificate is configured on the session. In tests, I mock the token endpoint, and I make token and secret rotation seamless by reading from the source on refresh.`,
    f: ["Why refresh before expiry, not only on 401?","How do you make token refresh thread-safe?"]
  },
  36: {
    a: `For a webhook receiver, such as one handling alerts or repository events, I use FastAPI or Flask to expose an endpoint that accepts a POST, verifies authenticity, validates the payload, acknowledges quickly and processes asynchronously. Verification means checking a signature header, an HMAC of the body with a shared secret compared in constant time, or a bearer token, and rejecting anything else, since the endpoint is exposed. FastAPI uses pydantic models to validate the body automatically and generates documentation, and supports async handlers. Long work is handed to a background task or a queue and a worker, returning a response within a few seconds so senders do not time out and retry. I make handlers idempotent using delivery IDs, because webhooks can be delivered more than once. Production concerns: run under a proper server such as uvicorn or gunicorn, add health and readiness endpoints, structured logging, metrics, request size limits, TLS at the ingress, rate limiting and tests with the framework's test client.`,
    f: ["Why acknowledge the webhook quickly?","How do you verify an HMAC signature safely?"]
  },
  37: {
    a: `Both platforms have REST and GraphQL APIs and official or community Python libraries such as PyGithub and python-gitlab, which wrap authentication, pagination and common objects. Typical automation includes enforcing branch protection and repository settings across an organisation, creating repositories from templates, opening pull requests for dependency or configuration updates across many repositories, auditing access and stale branches, labelling and triaging issues, and creating releases and tags from a pipeline. Authentication should use a GitHub App installation token or a fine-grained token with minimal permissions rather than a personal token, which is short-lived and auditable. I handle rate limits by reading the rate-limit headers and backing off, use conditional requests with ETags to save quota, and paginate everything. Changes are made through pull requests where possible so humans can review, and bulk operations run in dry-run first with a report. Webhooks trigger event-driven automation. Sensitive tokens live in the CI secret store, and results are logged for audit.`,
    f: ["Why prefer a GitHub App over a personal token?","How do you protect against hitting rate limits?"]
  },
  38: {
    a: `Most of these offer REST APIs and webhooks. For notifications, a Slack incoming webhook or the Web API posts messages to channels, and I format them with blocks, include links to runbooks, dashboards and the job that produced them, and keep them concise to avoid noise. For incidents, the PagerDuty Events API triggers, acknowledges and resolves alerts using a deduplication key, so repeated events update a single incident instead of creating many. For tickets, Jira, ServiceNow and others have APIs to create and update issues, which automation can use to record findings or track remediation. Interactive flows use Slack slash commands or buttons, calling back to a service that must verify the Slack signature and respond within a few seconds. Good practice: store tokens in a secrets manager, handle failures of the notification system without breaking the main job, rate limit messages, redact sensitive data in messages, use threads to group related updates, and make the integration idempotent so retries do not duplicate tickets.`,
    f: ["What is a dedup key in PagerDuty?","How do you verify a Slack request?"]
  },
  39: {
    a: `Data from outside the process cannot be trusted to match what the code expects, so I validate at the boundary. With pydantic, I declare a model describing the expected structure and types, and construct it from the raw dictionary, which converts compatible types, applies defaults, enforces constraints such as ranges, lengths and patterns, and raises a ValidationError listing every problem with its location. Custom validators handle cross-field rules, such as an end time after a start time. With JSON Schema, a language-neutral description validated by the jsonschema library, I can share the same schema with other systems and documentation, and it is the natural choice for configuration files and OpenAPI-defined payloads. Either way, I fail early with clear errors, log what failed, and decide per case whether to reject the whole input or skip invalid records and report them. Strict modes avoid surprising coercion. Models also serve as documentation and give editors completion, and I keep them versioned along with the API they represent.`,
    f: ["When would you choose JSON Schema over pydantic?","How do you handle partially invalid records?"]
  },
  40: {
    a: `I match the tool to the bottleneck. If work is I/O-bound, waiting on networks, APIs, SSH or disks, then threads or asyncio give concurrency, since the GIL is released during waiting. Threads are simplest to adopt with existing blocking libraries, using a ThreadPoolExecutor, and handle moderate concurrency of tens to hundreds of tasks. Asyncio uses a single thread with cooperative scheduling and scales to thousands of concurrent connections with low overhead, but needs async-compatible libraries such as aiohttp or httpx, and any blocking call stalls the event loop. If work is CPU-bound, parsing, compressing or computing, then multiprocessing or ProcessPoolExecutor uses multiple cores, at the cost of process start-up, serialisation of arguments and results, and memory. I also consider simplicity: for a script that calls fifty APIs, a thread pool is usually enough. Whichever I use, I bound concurrency with a limit, handle errors per task, and avoid shared mutable state without locks or queues.`,
    f: ["What blocks the asyncio event loop?","Why is process communication expensive?"]
  },
  41: {
    a: `Asyncio runs an event loop in one thread. Functions defined with async def are coroutines, and the await keyword hands control back to the loop while waiting for an operation, such as a network response, so other coroutines can progress. To run many calls concurrently, I create tasks, or use asyncio.gather or a TaskGroup, and use an async HTTP client like httpx or aiohttp with a shared client session. Concurrency must be bounded with a semaphore, since unlimited tasks can exhaust sockets, memory or rate limits. Timeouts are applied with asyncio.timeout or wait_for, errors are handled per task, and gather with return_exceptions collects failures without cancelling everything, whereas TaskGroup cancels siblings on error. The main pitfalls are calling blocking functions inside coroutines, which freezes the loop, solved by moving them to a thread with asyncio.to_thread, forgetting to await a coroutine, and mixing sync and async code carelessly. The entry point is asyncio.run.`,
    f: ["How do you limit concurrent requests?","What happens if you call time.sleep in a coroutine?"]
  },
  42: {
    a: `The concurrent.futures module makes this simple. I create a ThreadPoolExecutor with a limited number of workers, submit one task per host or use map, and collect results as they complete with as_completed, which lets me report progress and handle failures individually. Each future raises its exception when result is called, so I wrap the call and record failures without stopping the batch. Choosing the worker count matters: enough to overlap waiting, but low enough not to overwhelm the targets, a bastion or an API's rate limit. Tasks should be independent and return data rather than mutating shared structures, and if shared state is unavoidable, protect it with a lock or use queue-safe structures. Per-task timeouts must be implemented inside the task, for example on socket or HTTP calls, because a thread cannot be killed from outside. Using the executor as a context manager waits for completion and cleans up. I log which host each result belongs to and return a summary with an exit code reflecting failures.`,
    f: ["Why can a thread not be force-killed?","How do you report progress as tasks finish?"]
  },
  43: {
    a: `I measure before changing anything. For a quick view, cProfile with the sort option shows time per function, and visualisers such as snakeviz or flame graphs make it easier to read. For line-level detail, line_profiler shows time per line in a chosen function. Timing blocks with time.perf_counter or a context manager helps isolate phases like network, parsing and writing. For running processes, py-spy attaches without modifying code and produces flame graphs or dumps of what each thread is doing, which is ideal in production. Memory is checked with tracemalloc or memray. Typical findings are accidental quadratic algorithms such as repeated membership checks on lists, which become sets, work repeated inside loops, per-item network calls that could be batched, excessive logging, regex recompilation and serial calls that could be concurrent. After optimising, I rerun the profile to verify the improvement and add a benchmark if the performance is important. Premature optimisation is avoided: focus on the largest contributor first.`,
    f: ["What does py-spy let you do in production?","Why do membership tests on a list become slow?"]
  },
  44: {
    a: `Symptoms are growing resident memory, out-of-memory kills or slow garbage collection. First, determine whether memory grows steadily, indicating a leak or unbounded cache, or spikes from loading a large dataset. Tools: tracemalloc snapshots compare allocations between two points and show which lines allocated the growth, memray gives detailed profiles including native allocations, and objgraph helps find reference chains that keep objects alive. Typical causes: reading whole files or API result sets into lists instead of streaming, unbounded caches or dictionaries keyed by ever-growing values, global lists collecting results, references held by closures, logging handlers or callbacks, and circular references with finalisers. Fixes: stream with generators and chunks, use bounded caches such as functools.lru_cache with maxsize or a TTL cache, process in batches, use compact types such as arrays, slots in classes, or numpy and pandas dtypes, and release references. In containers, I set memory limits and watch the working set, and for long-running services, I monitor memory metrics and restart policies as a safety net, not as the fix.`,
    f: ["How do you compare two tracemalloc snapshots?","Why are unbounded caches dangerous?"]
  },
  45: {
    a: `I wrap the API in a small class so callers do not deal with URLs, headers and parsing. The constructor takes the base URL, credentials and a session, which makes it easy to inject a fake in tests. Methods map to API operations with clear names and typed arguments and return typed objects, such as dataclasses or pydantic models, not raw dictionaries, so errors appear at the boundary. Common concerns are centralised: authentication, timeouts, retries with backoff, pagination helpers, and translation of HTTP failures into a small hierarchy of exceptions, for example NotFound, RateLimited and ServerError, so callers can handle cases without inspecting status codes. Logging includes request IDs but excludes secrets. For testing, I use the responses or respx library to mock HTTP at the transport level, or a fake server fixture, and contract tests against a staging endpoint. I version the library alongside the API, document with docstrings and examples, and keep a changelog, so consumers can upgrade safely.`,
    f: ["Why return typed objects instead of dictionaries?","How do you mock HTTP calls in tests?"]
  },
  46: {
    a: `When work arrives continuously, or a stage produces items faster than the next can consume, a queue decouples them. The standard library queue module provides thread-safe queues, and multiprocessing and asyncio have their own versions. A producer puts items on the queue, several workers take items, process them and mark them done, and a sentinel value or a join call signals completion. A bounded queue size applies back-pressure: when full, the producer blocks, preventing memory growth if consumers are slow. Workers should catch exceptions per item so one failure does not kill the worker, and failed items can go to a retry or dead-letter queue. Graceful shutdown stops accepting new work, drains the queue and joins workers, and handles SIGTERM. For durable, distributed workloads across machines, an external broker with Celery, RQ or a cloud queue such as SQS is better, since in-process queues vanish on a crash. I add metrics on queue depth and processing time, which reveal bottlenecks and sizing needs.`,
    f: ["What does a bounded queue give you?","When do you need an external broker?"]
  },
  47: {
    a: `A race condition occurs when the result depends on the timing of threads accessing shared data, for example two threads incrementing a counter, because read-modify-write sequences are not atomic even with the GIL. Prevention starts with design: avoid shared mutable state, pass data through queues, and have each worker return results that the main thread combines. When sharing is needed, protect critical sections with a lock used as a context manager, keep them small, and use thread-safe structures. Deadlocks happen when threads wait for each other's locks, typically by acquiring several locks in different orders; the remedy is a consistent global lock ordering, using timeouts on acquire, or reducing to a single lock. Related problems are lock contention, which hurts performance, and forgetting to release locks on exceptions, avoided by with. For processes, shared memory needs explicit synchronisation, and file-based locks protect external resources. I also test concurrent code under load with randomised delays and use tools like thread dumps with faulthandler or py-spy to see where threads are stuck.`,
    f: ["Why is counter += 1 not thread-safe?","How do you find where a deadlocked process is stuck?"]
  },
  48: {
    a: `Caching stores the results of expensive calls so repeated calls with the same input return immediately. The functools.lru_cache decorator memoises a function by its arguments, which must be hashable, with a maxsize that bounds memory by evicting the least recently used entries, and cache_info shows hits and misses. The cache decorator is an unbounded variant. For data that goes stale, such as API results, a time-to-live is needed, available in libraries like cachetools or a manual timestamp, and for sharing across processes or runs, a disk or external cache like Redis. Pitfalls: caching functions with side effects or results that depend on hidden state, so stale or wrong values are returned; unbounded caches growing without limit; caching mutable return values that callers then modify, corrupting the cached object; lru_cache on instance methods, which keeps the instance alive through the cache; and forgetting invalidation. Cache only pure, expensive calls, set sizes and lifetimes deliberately, and expose a way to clear it.`,
    f: ["Why must arguments be hashable?","Why is lru_cache on a method risky?"]
  },
  49: {
    a: `I look for the biggest wins first. Algorithmic improvements usually dominate: replace repeated list searches with sets or dictionaries, avoid nested loops over large collections, sort and merge instead of comparing everything pairwise, and move invariant work out of loops. Use built-in functions and the standard library, which are implemented in C, such as sum, sorted, collections.Counter and itertools. For tabular numeric data, vectorised operations in pandas or numpy, or an analytical engine such as DuckDB or Polars, can be orders of magnitude faster than Python loops and can process files larger than memory with lazy evaluation. For CPU-bound parts that must stay in Python, split into chunks and use a process pool, being careful about serialisation costs. For hot numerical loops, Numba, Cython or rewriting in a compiled language through an extension can help. I/O matters too: read in large chunks, use efficient formats such as Parquet instead of CSV, and avoid per-row network calls. Measure after each change.`,
    f: ["When would DuckDB beat pandas?","What costs appear when using multiprocessing?"]
  },
  50: {
    a: `Pytest discovers functions named test_ in files named test_, and uses plain assert statements with detailed failure output. Fixtures provide reusable setup and teardown: a function decorated with pytest.fixture returns or yields a resource, such as a temporary directory, a fake client or a database, and tests request it by naming it as an argument. Scope controls how often it is created: per function, module or session. Fixtures can depend on other fixtures, and conftest files share them across a directory. Parametrisation runs the same test with many inputs through the parametrize marker, giving each case its own test result, which is ideal for edge cases such as parsing different log formats. Other useful features: tmp_path for temporary files, monkeypatch to set environment variables or replace functions, capsys and caplog to check output and logs, markers to group slow or integration tests, and plugins like pytest-cov and pytest-xdist. I keep tests small, deterministic and independent, naming them after the behaviour they verify.`,
    f: ["What does fixture scope control?","How does monkeypatch differ from mock?"]
  },
  51: {
    a: `Tests should not call real cloud accounts or APIs: they are slow, flaky, cost money and can change real resources. I choose the mocking level to fit. For AWS with boto3, the moto library simulates many services in memory, so code can create buckets, instances and queues against a fake backend, and assertions check the resulting state, which tests behaviour rather than call sequences. For HTTP APIs, responses or respx intercept requests at the transport level and return canned responses, including errors and rate limits, so I can test retry logic. For arbitrary dependencies, unittest.mock's patch replaces objects where they are used, and MagicMock records calls so I can assert on arguments, but over-mocking couples tests to implementation details. I design code with dependency injection, passing the client in, which makes substitution simple. I also keep a small set of integration tests against a real sandbox account, run less frequently, to catch differences between mocks and reality.`,
    f: ["Where should you patch: where defined or where used?","Why add a few real integration tests?"]
  },
  52: {
    a: `I separate logic from the command line layer: the main behaviour lives in importable functions that take parameters and return results, tested directly, while the CLI module only parses arguments and calls them. For the CLI layer, frameworks give test helpers: click and typer provide a CliRunner that invokes a command in-process and captures output and exit code, so I assert on both. With argparse, I call main with a list of arguments. For scripts that run subprocesses or touch the file system, I use tmp_path and monkeypatch to redirect paths and environment variables, and mock the subprocess boundary or use a fake executable placed on PATH. I test the unhappy paths: invalid input, missing files, permission errors, and that the exit code is nonzero with a helpful message. Dry-run modes are tested to verify nothing is changed. A small number of end-to-end tests run the installed command in a clean environment, which also proves packaging works. Output formats such as JSON are checked by parsing, not string matching.`,
    f: ["Why separate logic from argument parsing?","How do you assert exit codes in tests?"]
  },
  53: {
    a: `Coverage measures which lines or branches executed during tests, using coverage.py through pytest-cov. It shows untested code, but not whether tests assert anything meaningful, so a high number can be misleading, and I treat it as a guide for finding gaps, not as a goal in itself. Branch coverage is more informative than line coverage, since it checks both outcomes of conditions. In CI I generate a report and enforce a reasonable minimum threshold, such as the current level, preventing regression, with a rule that new code should be well covered, using diff coverage tools to focus on changes. I exclude generated code and unreachable defensive lines with comments, but sparingly. The report is published as an artifact and a pull request comment. For critical paths such as deletion logic and parsing, I aim for thorough tests including error cases, and consider mutation testing to check that tests catch changes. The aim is confidence, not a percentage, so I review uncovered lines in code review.`,
    f: ["Why is branch coverage better than line coverage?","Can 100 percent coverage still miss bugs?"]
  },
  54: {
    a: `They automate consistency and catch bugs before runtime. A formatter such as black or ruff format applies one style, ending debates and keeping diffs small. A linter such as ruff, which replaces many older tools like flake8, isort and pyupgrade, flags unused imports and variables, undefined names, risky patterns such as bare except, mutable defaults, subprocess with shell and insecure functions through its security rules, and can auto-fix many issues quickly. A type checker such as mypy or pyright analyses annotations to find mismatched types, missing returns and None handling errors, which are very common in scripts dealing with optional API fields. I configure everything in pyproject, run them in a pre-commit hook for instant feedback and again in CI as required checks, so nothing depends on a developer's setup. Adoption in an existing code base is gradual: enable a basic rule set, fix or baseline existing issues, then tighten. Security-focused additions include bandit rules and dependency auditing.`,
    f: ["Why run the same checks in pre-commit and CI?","How do you adopt mypy in an untyped code base?"]
  },
  55: {
    a: `Such code is hard to test because results change between runs, so I make the dependencies explicit and controllable. For time, I avoid calling datetime.now deep inside functions; instead, I pass a clock or the current time as a parameter, or wrap it in a small function that tests can replace, or use a library such as freezegun or time-machine to freeze and advance time, which lets me test expiry, scheduling and retry delays instantly, without sleeping. For randomness, I seed the random generator or inject it, and for jitter I test properties such as bounds rather than exact values. For external state such as environment variables, the file system and the network, I use monkeypatch, temporary directories and fakes, and reset state between tests so they are independent and can run in any order or in parallel. For tests of retry and backoff logic, I replace sleep with a recorder, asserting on the delays requested. Flaky tests are fixed or quarantined promptly.`,
    f: ["How do you test a backoff schedule without waiting?","Why inject a clock?"]
  },
  56: {
    a: `I use a pyramid. At the base, fast unit tests cover pure logic: parsing, decision rules, formatting and validation, with no cloud access. Above that, component tests exercise modules against fakes such as moto for AWS and responses for HTTP, verifying behaviour including error and retry paths. Then integration tests run against a real but disposable sandbox account, project or cluster, created for the pipeline and destroyed afterwards, to confirm permissions, API quirks and eventual consistency issues that mocks hide, kept few and focused. For destructive tools, tests prove that dry-run changes nothing and that safeguards, such as limits and tag checks, prevent deleting the wrong things, since the worst bugs are destruction of production data. Tests assert idempotency by running operations twice. Contract tests compare mocks with real API responses periodically. In CI, unit and component tests run on every commit, integration tests on merge or nightly, with cost and cleanup controls and unique resource prefixes to avoid collisions.`,
    f: ["Which tests prove a delete tool is safe?","How do you prevent leftover resources from integration tests?"]
  },
  57: {
    a: `Pre-commit runs fast checks locally before each commit, defined in a configuration file that lists hooks from repositories pinned by version. Typical hooks include ruff for lint and format, mypy, detection of private keys and secrets, trailing whitespace and end-of-file fixers, YAML and TOML validation, and a check against large files. Because hooks are versioned in the repository, every developer gets the same setup after installing once. They are quick, so they do not become an obstacle. CI repeats the same checks, since local hooks can be skipped, and adds slower ones: the full test suite with coverage, dependency vulnerability scanning with pip-audit, a build of the package, container image scanning if relevant, and integration tests on merge. Branch protection requires the checks to pass, and automated dependency updates through Renovate or Dependabot propose upgrades with the tests as the safety net. A README documents how to run the checks locally with one command, so contributors can reproduce CI failures.`,
    f: ["Why must CI repeat pre-commit checks?","How does pip-audit help?"]
  },
  58: {
    a: `Testinfra is a pytest plugin that lets you write tests about the state of a server: that a package is installed, a service is running and enabled, a port is listening, a file exists with given permissions and content, or a command returns a certain output. It connects through local execution, SSH, Docker or Ansible inventory, so the same tests can validate a container, a VM built by Packer, or hosts provisioned by Ansible. Tests use a host fixture with objects such as host.package, host.service, host.socket and host.file, which makes them readable. I use it to verify images before publishing them, to check that a configuration run achieved the intended state, and as smoke tests after deployments. It complements, but does not replace, application-level tests. Similar tools include Goss and InSpec. Tests should check observable outcomes, not implementation details, run quickly and fail with clear messages, and be part of the pipeline that builds and releases the infrastructure.`,
    f: ["Which connection backends does Testinfra support?","How is this different from application tests?"]
  },
  59: {
    a: `A virtual environment is an isolated directory with its own Python interpreter link and site-packages, so each project installs its own dependency versions without affecting the system Python or other projects. The venv module is built in, virtualenv is a faster third-party tool with extra features, and tools like uv create environments extremely quickly. Activating an environment adjusts the path so python and pip refer to it, though calling the interpreter by its full path works without activation, which is how cron jobs and systemd units should run them. Pipx installs command-line tools each in its own isolated environment and exposes the commands, ideal for tools like black or awscli-like utilities. Uv is a modern, fast package and project manager that can create environments, resolve and lock dependencies, manage Python versions and run tools, replacing pip, pip-tools, virtualenv and pipx in many workflows. On servers, I never install project dependencies into the system Python, since operating system tools may depend on it and upgrades can break them.`,
    f: ["Why avoid installing into the system Python?","How do you run a venv script from cron?"]
  },
  60: {
    a: `Pip installs packages from an index, and a requirements file lists them, but on its own it does not lock transitive dependencies, so two installs on different days can differ. Pip-tools compiles a loose requirements input file into a fully pinned requirements file with hashes, giving reproducible installs with a simple workflow. Poetry manages a project with pyproject, resolves dependencies, creates a lock file, builds and publishes packages, and handles virtual environments, with a more opinionated workflow. Uv is a newer, very fast tool that provides a pip-compatible interface and a project workflow with a cross-platform lock file, plus Python installation and tool running. The principle is the same: declare direct dependencies with sensible version ranges in pyproject, generate a lock file that pins everything, including hashes, install from the lock in CI and production, and update deliberately through a pull request, ideally automated. The choice depends on team preference, speed needs and tooling support, but consistency within the organisation matters more than which tool is chosen.`,
    f: ["Why include hashes in a lock file?","What is the difference between direct and transitive dependencies?"]
  },
  61: {
    a: `The pyproject file is the standard place for project metadata and tool configuration. The build-system table names the build backend, such as hatchling, setuptools or flit, and its requirements. The project table holds the name, version, description, required Python version, dependencies, optional dependency groups for extras like dev or docs, and scripts that define command-line entry points. Tool tables configure ruff, mypy, pytest and coverage in one file. A common layout uses a src directory containing the package, which prevents accidentally importing the local folder instead of the installed package and catches packaging mistakes, with a tests directory beside it, a README, a licence, a changelog and CI configuration. Version can be static or derived from Git tags. The project is installed in editable mode for development, and built into a wheel for release. Keeping configuration in one standard file reduces clutter and makes the project understandable to new contributors and tooling.`,
    f: ["What does the src layout protect against?","What are optional dependencies?"]
  },
  62: {
    a: `Third-party packages run with the privileges of the script, so the supply chain is an attack surface. I start by minimising dependencies and choosing well-maintained ones. I pin exact versions with hashes in a lock file and install with hash checking, so a tampered or substituted package fails verification. I prefer an internal package index or proxy that mirrors approved packages, so builds do not depend on the public index and typosquatted or dependency-confusion names, where a public package shadows an internal name, are blocked by configuring index priority and reserving internal names. Vulnerability scanning with pip-audit, Dependabot or similar runs in CI and on a schedule, and automated update pull requests keep versions current. I generate a software bill of materials for releases, use trusted publishing and signing for packages I publish, and run builds in clean environments with least privilege and no long-lived secrets. Reviewing the changelog of major updates and watching for suspicious maintainer changes completes the picture.`,
    f: ["What is dependency confusion?","What does hash-checking mode do?"]
  },
  63: {
    a: `I start from a slim official base image matching the Python version, or a distroless image for the final stage, and use a multi-stage build: the first stage installs build tools and compiles dependencies into wheels or a virtual environment, and the final stage copies only the environment and application code, keeping the image small and free of compilers. Dependencies are installed from the lock file before copying the source, so Docker's layer cache is reused when only code changes. I run as a non-root user, set environment variables like PYTHONUNBUFFERED so logs appear immediately and PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE to avoid clutter, use a read-only filesystem where possible, and define a health check. Secrets are never baked into the image or build arguments, but supplied at run time. The process handles SIGTERM, runs as PID one directly through an exec-form command, or under a minimal init, so shutdown is clean. I pin the base image by digest, scan the image for vulnerabilities and rebuild regularly for patches.`,
    f: ["Why copy the lock file before the source code?","Why use the exec form of CMD?"]
  },
  64: {
    a: `The project declares the supported Python versions in pyproject with requires-python, and CI tests across them with a matrix, so compatibility is verified. For developers, version managers such as pyenv or uv install and switch interpreters per project, with a version file in the repository like python-version, so everyone uses the same one. On servers, I avoid relying on the system Python, which belongs to the operating system and changes with upgrades, and instead use containers with a pinned Python, or a dedicated interpreter installed under an application directory or by a configuration management role, with a virtual environment built on it. When an operating system upgrade removes or changes a Python version, applications that depend on it break, which is why dedicated interpreters and containers are safer. I track end-of-life dates, since versions stop receiving security patches after about five years, and plan upgrades, testing with the new version in CI early and fixing deprecation warnings, which can be turned into errors in tests.`,
    f: ["Why not rely on the system Python?","How do you catch deprecations before an upgrade?"]
  },
  65: {
    a: `There are several approaches depending on constraints. A wheelhouse is a directory of wheels for the application and all its dependencies, built on a matching platform and Python version with pip download or pip wheel, copied to the target and installed with no-index and find-links, which works offline and reproducibly. A zipapp, created with the zipapp module, bundles pure Python code and dependencies into one runnable file, simple when dependencies are pure Python. PyInstaller or similar tools freeze the application and an interpreter into a single executable, which avoids needing Python on the target but is larger and platform specific. A container image delivered through an internal registry or tarball is another option when a container runtime exists. Finally, an internal package mirror such as devpi, Artifactory or Nexus lets hosts install normally from inside the network. Whichever I use, I sign or checksum artefacts, version them, and ensure the build platform matches the target's architecture and libc.`,
    f: ["What must match between build and target platforms?","When would you choose PyInstaller?"]
  },
  66: {
    a: `I use semantic versioning: patch for bug fixes, minor for backward compatible features and major for breaking changes, with the version derived from a Git tag by the build backend so there is a single source of truth. A changelog records user-visible changes. The release flow is automated: when a tag is pushed, CI runs the test suite, builds the sdist and wheel with the standard build tool, verifies the package with twine check, and uploads to the internal index, such as Artifactory, Nexus, a cloud artifact registry or a private PyPI, using short-lived credentials or trusted publishing, not a long-lived token stored in plain text. Consumers pin version ranges, and I keep old releases available, yanking bad ones rather than deleting them. For breaking changes I provide deprecation warnings one release ahead, and document the migration. Documentation is published alongside, and the package includes metadata, a licence and a typed marker file so that type checkers can use the annotations.`,
    f: ["What is the difference between yanking and deleting a release?","What is a py.typed marker?"]
  },
  67: {
    a: `Multiprocessing runs separate interpreter processes, so CPU-bound Python code can use several cores. The ProcessPoolExecutor or multiprocessing.Pool distributes tasks to worker processes. Arguments and results are pickled and sent between processes, so they must be picklable, and large objects are expensive to transfer, which means chunking work and returning small results is important, or sharing data through memory-mapped files or shared memory. Functions must be importable at module level, and scripts need the main guard, because on some platforms new processes import the main module again, and without the guard they spawn recursively. The start method matters: fork is fast but unsafe with threads and some libraries, while spawn is safer and the default on macOS and Windows. Choose the worker count near the number of cores, leaving headroom, and consider memory since each process has its own copy. Handle worker exceptions by checking future results, set timeouts and make sure the pool shuts down cleanly. For many small tasks, overhead can exceed the gain, so measure before adopting.`,
    f: ["Why does the main guard matter with multiprocessing?","What must be true of arguments passed to workers?"]
  },
  68: {
    a: `Property-based testing, with libraries such as Hypothesis, generates many inputs automatically and checks that stated properties hold for all of them, instead of testing a few hand-picked examples. You describe the kinds of input, such as strings, integers in a range or structured records, and a property, such as parsing then formatting a value gives the original back, a sorted list is ordered, or a function never raises for valid input. When a failure is found, the tool shrinks the input to a minimal failing example, which makes the bug easy to understand. It is valuable for parsers of logs and configuration formats, CIDR and version-string handling, serialisation, rate limiters and idempotency, where edge cases such as empty strings, unusual Unicode, huge numbers or boundaries are easy to overlook. It complements example-based tests, which remain better for documenting specific scenarios. Practical points are keeping properties meaningful, seeding for reproducibility in CI, controlling runtime with settings, and saving found failures as regression tests.`,
    f: ["What does shrinking do?","Which properties are good for a parser?"]
  },
  69: {
    a: `I install the application into a dedicated directory with its own virtual environment built from the lock file, owned by a service account, rather than into the system Python. A systemd unit then runs it: ExecStart points at the environment's interpreter or console script, User and Group set the unprivileged account, WorkingDirectory and Environment or EnvironmentFile supply configuration, and Restart on failure with a limited restart rate handles crashes. Hardening options such as NoNewPrivileges, ProtectSystem, PrivateTmp, ReadOnlyPaths and memory or CPU limits reduce risk. Logs go to standard output and are collected by the journal, with structured fields. For periodic jobs, a systemd timer pairs with a oneshot service and gives better logging, dependency handling, randomised delay and missed-run catch-up than cron. Deployments create a new versioned directory and switch a symlink, so rollback is quick, with configuration management or a pipeline doing the work, and a health check after restart. Graceful shutdown handles SIGTERM, within TimeoutStopSec.`,
    f: ["Why use a systemd timer instead of cron?","Which systemd options harden a service?"]
  },
  70: {
    a: `For simple tasks, the csv module reads and writes rows as lists or dictionaries with DictReader and DictWriter, streams large files and needs no dependency. For analysis, pandas loads data into DataFrames where I can filter, group, join and pivot with concise operations, and read and write CSV, JSON, Excel and Parquet. Typical reports are cost by tag from billing exports, inventory of instances by region, certificate expiry lists or vulnerability summaries. I specify dtypes and date parsing explicitly, use chunksize or usecols for big files, handle missing values deliberately and avoid iterating rows in Python, preferring vectorised operations. For very large data, DuckDB or Polars query files directly with SQL or lazy plans, and are fast and memory efficient. Output should be reproducible and reviewable: sorted rows, stable column order, timestamps in UTC, and clear headers. Reports can be written to CSV or Excel, rendered as HTML or Markdown tables and sent by email or Slack, with sensitive columns removed and the generating code and parameters recorded.`,
    f: ["Why avoid iterating rows in pandas?","When would you use DuckDB instead of pandas?"]
  },
  71: {
    a: `The script should be simple, fast and honest about failure. It checks one or a few well-defined things, such as an HTTP endpoint returning the expected status and content within a time limit, a TCP port, disk usage on a mount, certificate expiry or a queue depth, with explicit timeouts on every network call so it cannot hang and pile up. It reports results through the exit code, a metric or a push to the monitoring system, and a structured log line, and distinguishes check failed from check could not run. To avoid alert storms, it applies thresholds with a few consecutive failures before alerting, and recovery notifications. A lock file or flock prevents overlapping runs if one is slow. It runs as an unprivileged user with minimal access, and credentials come from the environment or a secrets store. I prefer exposing metrics to Prometheus, through a textfile collector or an exporter, and alerting centrally, rather than sending ad-hoc emails from each script.`,
    f: ["How do you prevent overlapping cron runs?","How do you distinguish a failing check from a broken check?"]
  },
  72: {
    a: `Cleanup scripts are dangerous because mistakes delete real data, so safety dominates the design. I begin with discovery only: list candidates, such as unattached volumes older than a threshold, snapshots without an associated AMI or retention tag, or idle load balancers, and output a report with owner tags, ages and estimated cost. The deletion step is separate, requires an explicit apply flag and defaults to dry-run. Multiple guards apply: skip resources tagged with a do-not-delete or retention marker, require a minimum age, restrict to specific accounts and regions printed before running, cap the number of deletions per run, and snapshot or back up before deleting where appropriate. A grace period approach first tags a resource with a planned deletion date and notifies the owner, deleting later only if no one objects. I use paginators for complete listing, handle dependencies and errors per resource without stopping, log every action with identifiers for audit, and make the job idempotent. Testing runs in a sandbox account with decoy resources.`,
    f: ["What is a grace period or tag-then-delete approach?","Which guards limit the blast radius?"]
  },
  73: {
    a: `Expired certificates cause avoidable outages, so a scheduled check should inventory endpoints and warn well ahead. For each host and port, the script opens a TLS connection with the ssl module and a timeout, uses the proper server name for SNI, retrieves the peer certificate and reads the notAfter date, computing days remaining. Libraries such as cryptography parse certificates in more detail, including issuer, subject alternative names and chain, useful for finding wrong or incomplete chains. Sources of endpoints include a static list, DNS records, load balancers, Kubernetes ingress resources and certificate manager inventories, plus certificate files on disk for internal services. Thresholds create tiered alerts, for example warning at thirty days and critical at seven, and the check emits a Prometheus metric with the expiry timestamp so alert rules and dashboards work centrally. It must verify the certificate properly for trust as well, and distinguish connection failures from expiry. Where possible, automation with ACME or a certificate manager renews certificates, and the check confirms renewal actually happened.`,
    f: ["Why must you set SNI when connecting?","What other certificate problems besides expiry should you detect?"]
  },
  74: {
    a: `A backup script orchestrates native tools rather than reimplementing them. It runs pg_dump or a physical backup tool, or triggers cloud snapshots through the SDK, with credentials from the environment or a secrets manager, uses compression and encryption, streams output directly to object storage to avoid large temporary files, and writes a checksum and a manifest with the timestamp, version, size and source. It applies a retention policy, deleting old backups according to rules, with protection for the most recent good backups, and sends metrics and alerts if a run fails or is late. Crucially, backups are only real if they restore, so a verification job periodically restores the latest backup to an isolated instance, runs sanity queries or row count comparisons, measures restore time against the recovery objective, and reports the result. Locking prevents overlapping runs, errors from the subprocess are checked through exit codes and not ignored, and logs omit secrets. Access to backup storage is restricted and immutable or versioned storage protects against ransomware and mistakes.`,
    f: ["Why must a backup be tested by restoring?","How do you protect backups from deletion?"]
  },
  75: {
    a: `I design it as a pipeline of collection, normalisation and reporting. Collection iterates through accounts, using an assumed role in each, and through enabled regions, calling the relevant describe or list APIs with paginators, in parallel with a bounded thread pool and error handling per account and region, so one failure does not abort the run. Retries and backoff handle throttling. The raw results are normalised into a common schema with fields like account, region, resource type, identifier, tags, creation time and key settings, which makes querying uniform across services. The data is stored, for example as Parquet or in a database, with a timestamp for each run, so I can see trends and changes. Rules then evaluate compliance, such as missing owner tags, unencrypted volumes, public buckets or outdated runtimes, producing findings with severity and owners. Reports are generated as dashboards or files and delivered to teams. Native services such as AWS Config or Resource Explorer can do part of this, so I use them when they fit, and write custom code for gaps.`,
    f: ["Why normalise into a common schema?","When would you use AWS Config instead of custom code?"]
  },
  76: {
    a: `A controller watches resources and reconciles actual state toward desired state. With kopf, a Python framework, I define handlers with decorators for events such as creation, update, deletion and timers on a custom resource, and the framework handles watching, retries, status patches, finalizers and leader election. First I define the custom resource definition with a validated schema, then write handlers that read the spec, create or patch child resources such as deployments or secrets, set owner references so Kubernetes garbage-collects children, and update the status with conditions that users can inspect. Handlers must be idempotent, since events can be repeated or replayed, and should not block for long. RBAC is least privilege for exactly the resources the operator manages, and it runs as a deployment with health probes and metrics. I test handlers with unit tests on the logic and an integration test in a kind cluster. For serious or widely used operators, Go with controller-runtime is the common choice, but Python is productive for internal automation.`,
    f: ["Why must handlers be idempotent?","What are owner references for?"]
  },
  77: {
    a: `The bot is a thin, audited front end over well-tested automation. Engineers invoke commands such as a status check or a scale operation through a slash command or button, which Slack sends to a service as a signed HTTP request. The service verifies the Slack signature and timestamp, acknowledges within three seconds and processes asynchronously, posting results back into the thread. Authorisation is essential: map the Slack user to roles, check permissions per command, and require approvals for risky actions with a second person confirming through an interactive message. Commands are an allow list, each backed by a function with validated arguments, not free-form shell execution. The bot uses its own scoped credentials, never the user's, and logs who ran what, when and with which parameters, to an audit trail, linking to the run output. Rate limits, per-channel restrictions and a kill switch limit damage. Idempotency keys prevent double execution on retries, and long operations report progress. Secrets stay out of messages, and outputs are redacted before posting.`,
    f: ["Why verify the Slack signature?","How do approvals work for risky commands?"]
  },
  78: {
    a: `Cron is simple and ubiquitous: a schedule line runs a command, but it has minimal environment, no dependency handling, little visibility, mail-based output and no built-in protection from overlapping runs or missed schedules when the machine is down. Systemd timers improve on this with journald logging, resource controls, dependencies, randomised delay, persistent catch-up after downtime and easy inspection with systemctl. Kubernetes CronJobs run containers on a schedule with concurrency policies, history limits, retries through the Job controller and the cluster's logging, good when the workload is containerised, though time zones, missed-start deadlines and idempotency need care. Workflow schedulers such as Airflow, Prefect or Dagster model multi-step pipelines with dependencies, retries, backfills, parameters, dashboards, alerting and per-task logs, suited to data pipelines and complex workflows, with more infrastructure to run. I choose the simplest tool that meets the need: timers for single-host jobs, CronJobs for cluster tasks, and a workflow engine when dependencies, backfills and visibility matter. Jobs should always be idempotent, lock against overlap and alert when they fail or do not run.`,
    f: ["What does a CronJob concurrencyPolicy do?","How do you detect a job that silently did not run?"]
  },
  79: {
    a: `Overlap protection on one host uses a lock file with flock, or the scheduler's own setting such as a Kubernetes concurrencyPolicy of Forbid, or systemd, which does not start a unit that is already running. Across machines, a distributed lock is needed: options include a database row or advisory lock, a lease in Redis with an expiring key, a compare-and-swap in etcd or Consul, or leader election, where one instance is chosen to run the scheduled work while others stand by. Locks should have a time-to-live and be renewed while the job runs, so a crashed holder does not block forever, and the job should tolerate the lock expiring by being idempotent, since clock pauses and network partitions can cause two holders. True exactly-once is hard, so I aim for at-least-once execution with idempotent effects, using idempotency keys or state records. Fencing tokens protect against a stale holder writing after losing the lock. Simpler alternatives avoid the problem: run the scheduler as a single instance with failover, or use a managed scheduler that guarantees single invocation.`,
    f: ["Why do distributed locks need a time-to-live?","What is a fencing token?"]
  },
  80: {
    a: `I start with detection: use psutil or shutil.disk_usage to read usage per mount, and report both percent and absolute free space, including inode usage, which can run out separately. Thresholds alert in tiers and export metrics so trends and time-to-full forecasts are visible. For cleanup, the safest approach is configured rotation, logrotate or journald limits, and fixing the source of excess logging, instead of a script deleting files. If an automated cleaner is needed, it works from an explicit allow list of directories and patterns, only removes files older than a retention period, never follows symlinks outside its scope, skips files that are open, which I can check with lsof or psutil, and logs everything it removes, with a dry-run mode. Truncating an active log is preferable to deleting it, since a deleted file held open by a process continues consuming space until the process closes it. After cleaning, it verifies free space increased and escalates if not. Testing uses a temporary directory with fake files before it touches a real system.`,
    f: ["Why does deleting an open file not free space?","Why monitor inodes as well as bytes?"]
  },
  81: {
    a: `I model it as an ordered, observable procedure. First, build the host list from a trusted source and divide it into small batches, never touching more than a safe fraction of capacity at once. For each batch: remove hosts from the load balancer or mark them draining and wait for connections to complete, perform the action, such as deploying a release and restarting the service, run health checks that must pass within a timeout, then re-add the hosts and watch error rate and latency from monitoring before moving on. If checks fail, the rollout stops automatically and optionally rolls back the affected batch, with a clear report. The first batch is a canary. Operations use idempotent steps, timeouts and retries, a lock to avoid two rollouts at once, and a dry-run showing the plan. Parallelism is bounded within a batch. Logs record each step per host. For standard cases, I would use existing tools, Ansible, Kubernetes rolling updates or a deployment platform, and only write custom code for logic those tools cannot express.`,
    f: ["What stops the rollout automatically?","When would you not write this in Python?"]
  },
  82: {
    a: `With the prometheus_client library I define metrics and expose them. A counter tracks events that only go up, such as requests or errors, a gauge tracks values that move up and down like queue depth, a histogram records distributions such as request latency in buckets, which allows percentile estimates in queries, and a summary is less often used. Labels add dimensions, but each unique label combination creates a new time series, so I avoid high-cardinality values such as user ids, URLs with ids or timestamps. The library can start an HTTP server on a port serving the metrics endpoint, or integrate with the web framework through middleware. Long-running services are scraped by Prometheus. Short-lived batch jobs cannot be scraped reliably, so they either write to the node exporter textfile collector or push to a Pushgateway, used only for such jobs, with a last-success timestamp metric. Naming follows conventions: base units such as seconds and bytes, a suffix of total for counters, and a clear help string. I then add alert rules and dashboards for the metrics.`,
    f: ["Why is label cardinality a problem?","How do batch jobs export metrics?"]
  },
  83: {
    a: `Structured logging emits each event as machine-readable fields, usually JSON, so a log platform can filter and aggregate by field rather than parsing text. With the standard logging module, I configure a formatter that outputs JSON, using python-json-logger or structlog, and add context such as timestamp in UTC, level, logger name, message, service, version, environment and a request or correlation id. Context variables or logging adapters attach the correlation id automatically across a request, including in async code. Exceptions are logged with a traceback in a field, using logger.exception. I log to standard output in containers, letting the platform collect them, and configure levels through the environment so debug can be enabled temporarily. Good practice includes consistent field names, avoiding secrets and personal data through redaction filters, not logging inside tight loops, sampling noisy events and keeping messages stable and descriptive with variable data in fields. Libraries should not configure handlers; the application does, at startup.`,
    f: ["Why a correlation id?","Where should handlers be configured?"]
  },
  84: {
    a: `OpenTelemetry provides APIs and SDKs for traces, metrics and logs with vendor-neutral export. For tracing, I install the SDK and instrumentation packages for the libraries in use, such as Flask, FastAPI, requests, SQLAlchemy and boto3, and either run the zero-code opentelemetry-instrument wrapper or initialise a tracer provider in code. A resource describes the service name, version and environment. Spans are created automatically for incoming requests and outgoing calls, and I add manual spans around important business operations with attributes such as customer tier or batch size, avoiding sensitive data. Context propagation passes the trace id through HTTP headers using the W3C trace context standard, so spans from different services join into a single trace. Spans are exported over OTLP to a collector, which batches, samples, filters and forwards to a backend such as Tempo or Jaeger. Sampling controls volume and cost, for example head sampling by ratio or tail sampling to keep errors and slow traces. Trace ids also go into logs, linking logs with traces.`,
    f: ["How does context propagation work between services?","What is the collector for?"]
  },
  85: {
    a: `A liveness endpoint answers whether the process is running and not stuck; it should be cheap and must not check dependencies, otherwise a database outage makes the orchestrator restart every healthy instance and worsen the incident. A readiness endpoint answers whether this instance can serve traffic now, so it can check that startup finished, caches are warm and critical dependencies are reachable, with short timeouts, and it returns a failing status so the load balancer stops routing to it without killing it. A startup probe protects slow-starting applications. During shutdown, readiness should fail first so traffic drains while in-flight requests finish. Responses use proper status codes, with a small body listing component status for humans, but no sensitive details, and endpoints are not exposed publicly. Checks should not be expensive or trigger heavy load, so I cache results for a few seconds. In Kubernetes, I configure the probes with sensible periods, thresholds and timeouts. Deeper dependency checks belong in monitoring and synthetic tests more than in liveness.`,
    f: ["Why should liveness not check the database?","What happens to readiness during shutdown?"]
  },
  86: {
    a: `A small web service, using FastAPI or Flask, receives the JSON payload that Alertmanager posts. It authenticates the sender through a shared secret, basic authentication, mutual TLS or network restrictions, validates the payload against a schema, and responds quickly with a success code, moving slow work to a background task or queue so Alertmanager does not time out and retry unnecessarily. The payload groups alerts with status firing or resolved, labels and annotations, so the handler maps labels like service and severity to actions: create or update a ticket, post to chat, trigger a runbook automation, or open an incident. Deduplication uses the alert fingerprint or group key so repeated notifications update the same item rather than creating duplicates, and resolved alerts close or annotate it. Any automated remediation is limited to safe, idempotent actions with rate limits and a kill switch, and all actions are logged. The receiver itself is monitored, because a silent broken alert path is dangerous, with a dead-man's-switch alert confirming the path works end to end.`,
    f: ["How do you avoid duplicate tickets?","What is a dead-man's switch alert?"]
  },
  87: {
    a: `I choose the approach by size. For streaming, I read the file line by line, or use generators, parse each line with a compiled regular expression or a JSON parser, and aggregate counts in dictionaries or collections.Counter, which uses constant memory and handles gigabytes. For richer analysis, I parse into a pandas DataFrame, converting timestamps to datetime, setting dtypes, and using categorical types for repeated strings, then resample by time, group by status, endpoint or host, compute percentiles and find top errors. Large files are read in chunks with chunksize, and compressed files are read directly. For bigger data, DuckDB or Polars run SQL or lazy queries over files quickly. I handle malformed lines deliberately by counting and logging them rather than failing. Typical outputs are error rates over time, slowest endpoints, spikes after deployments and top client addresses, shown as tables or charts. For ongoing needs, a log platform such as Loki or Elasticsearch is better than ad-hoc scripts, and scripts are suited to investigation.`,
    f: ["How do you keep memory low on a huge file?","When is a log platform better than a script?"]
  },
  88: {
    a: `An SLI is a measured ratio such as successful requests divided by total requests, or the fraction of requests faster than a latency threshold, and the SLO is the target for it over a window, for example 99.9 percent over thirty days. The error budget is the allowed failure, here 0.1 percent. To compute it, I query the metrics backend, for example the Prometheus HTTP API, for good and total event counts over the window, handle gaps and counter resets, and calculate the ratio and the remaining budget. Burn rate is the speed of budget consumption relative to the allowed rate: a burn rate of one uses exactly the budget over the window, while fourteen would exhaust a month in about two days. Multi-window, multi-burn-rate alerts combine a long and a short window, for example one hour and five minutes, to catch fast burns quickly and avoid noisy alerts. A script can produce weekly reports showing attainment, budget remaining and top contributors to errors, used to guide decisions about feature work versus reliability work. In practice, recording rules and tools such as Sloth generate this in Prometheus.`,
    f: ["What does a burn rate of 14 mean?","Why use two windows in an alert?"]
  },
  89: {
    a: `I begin non-invasively. Check system metrics for CPU, memory, file descriptors and I/O wait, and look at logs and traces for what changed. To see what the process is doing right now, py-spy dumps the stack of every thread of a running process without restarting it or modifying code, and records a flame graph of where time goes, with low overhead. The faulthandler module, enabled at startup, prints tracebacks on a fatal signal or on a timer or a user signal, which is valuable for hangs and deadlocks. For memory growth, tracemalloc compares snapshots to find allocation sites, and objgraph helps find reference cycles or leaks. For async programs, the event loop debug mode and task dumps show blocked coroutines, commonly a blocking call inside async code. Typical causes are missing network timeouts, lock contention, a thread waiting on a queue, unbounded caches and large payloads. Once identified, I reproduce in a test, fix, and add a metric or alert and timeouts so it is visible next time. Attaching a debugger to production is a last resort.`,
    f: ["What does py-spy do without restarting?","Which tool finds memory allocation sites?"]
  },
  90: {
    a: `When no exporter provides the data, I write a small one. For a long-running exporter, I use prometheus_client with a custom collector class whose collect method queries the source on each scrape and yields metric families, so values are always fresh and not stale from a background loop. It must be fast, apply timeouts to the upstream call, and report a scrape success gauge and duration, so failure to reach the source is visible and not silently showing old numbers. Metric names follow conventions, labels are low cardinality, and units are base units. For batch or cron-driven data, a script writes a file in Prometheus text format to the node exporter textfile directory, written atomically through a temporary file and rename, with a last-run timestamp so alerts can detect staleness. The exporter runs as an unprivileged service, listens on a limited interface and has its own alerts. Before building it, I check whether a community exporter or the blackbox exporter already covers the need. Documentation and tests of the output format help future maintainers.`,
    f: ["Why write the textfile atomically?","Why report a scrape success metric?"]
  },
  91: {
    a: `Retries recover from transient failures such as timeouts, connection resets, throttling and server errors. The delay between attempts grows exponentially, for example one, two, four, eight seconds, up to a maximum, and jitter adds randomness so many clients do not retry in lockstep and overload a recovering service. I limit total attempts and total elapsed time, honour a Retry-After header, and retry only errors known to be transient: network errors, status 429 and 5xx. I do not retry client errors such as bad requests or authorisation failures, or operations that are not safe to repeat. Operations should be idempotent, or carry an idempotency key, so a retried request that actually succeeded the first time does not duplicate effects. Libraries such as tenacity, backoff, urllib3 Retry and the boto3 retry modes provide this, so I avoid hand-rolled loops. Each retry is logged or counted in a metric, since silent retries hide a degrading dependency, and a circuit breaker stops retries when a dependency is clearly down.`,
    f: ["Why add jitter?","Which errors should not be retried?"]
  },
  92: {
    a: `A circuit breaker protects a caller and a failing dependency. It wraps calls and tracks failures. In the closed state calls go through normally. When failures exceed a threshold within a window, it opens, and calls fail immediately without contacting the dependency, which saves resources, avoids long waits and gives the dependency room to recover. After a cool-down it moves to half-open and lets a few trial calls through: if they succeed, it closes again, and if they fail it reopens. Together with timeouts, retries and bulkheads, it prevents cascading failures where threads pile up waiting on a dead service. In Python, libraries such as pybreaker or purgatory implement it, or it can be built with a small class. When open, the code needs a fallback: return cached data, a default, or a clear error, and report state changes in metrics and logs. Thresholds and cool-down times must be tuned to the dependency. For service-to-service traffic, a service mesh or proxy can also provide the pattern outside application code.`,
    f: ["What happens in the half-open state?","What should the caller do when the circuit is open?"]
  },
  93: {
    a: `An idempotent script can run repeatedly and reach the same end state, which matters because jobs are retried, interrupted and run twice. The pattern is to describe the desired state and compare it with the actual state: check whether the resource exists before creating it, update it only if it differs, and do nothing if it already matches. For cloud APIs, use natural identifiers or client tokens, such as an idempotency token on create calls, or deterministic names, and handle already-exists errors by treating them as success after verifying the settings. Use upsert operations where the API offers them, and prefer declarative tools such as Terraform or Ansible for this reason. Record progress in durable state so a partially completed run can resume, and design steps so each is safe to repeat. Deletions treat not-found as success. Avoid appending blindly to files or counters; instead write the whole intended content or use markers. Tests run the script twice and check the second run makes no changes, and a plan or dry-run mode shows intended actions.`,
    f: ["How do client tokens help?","How do you test idempotency?"]
  },
  94: {
    a: `Two concurrent runs of the same script can corrupt state or duplicate actions, so I use an explicit lock suited to the scope. On one host, fcntl.flock on a lock file is simple and is released automatically if the process dies, unlike a pid file that goes stale. The filelock library offers a portable wrapper. Across hosts, I use a lock in a shared system: a database advisory lock, a Redis key with set-if-not-exists and an expiry, a lease in etcd or Consul, or a cloud service such as DynamoDB conditional writes. Locks need a time-to-live and renewal so a crashed holder does not block forever, and the work should still be idempotent because a lock can expire or be lost during a pause. Fencing tokens let the protected resource reject stale writers. Lock scope should be as small as possible, held for the shortest time, with a timeout for acquisition and clear logging of who holds it. Where possible, I design work queues or leader election so locking is unnecessary.`,
    f: ["Why is flock better than a pid file?","What problem do fencing tokens solve?"]
  },
  95: {
    a: `I separate code from configuration, following the twelve-factor approach. Defaults live in code, environment-specific values come from environment variables or a config file, and secrets come from a secrets manager. A typed settings object, using pydantic-settings or dataclasses, loads and validates everything at startup, so a missing or malformed value fails immediately with a clear message, not halfway through a run. Precedence is clear and documented: command-line flags override environment variables, which override files, which override defaults. Config files use YAML, TOML or JSON, parsed with safe loaders such as yaml.safe_load, never executing code. Different environments such as development, staging and production use separate files or overlays, with no production values committed to the repository. I avoid reading configuration deep inside functions and instead pass a settings object in, which makes testing easy. Log the effective non-secret configuration at startup to help troubleshooting, and provide a validate or show-config command. Changes to configuration are reviewed like code.`,
    f: ["What is the config precedence order?","Why validate at startup?"]
  },
  96: {
    a: `Secrets never belong in source code, command-line arguments, which are visible in process lists, or logs. Preferred sources are workload identity, such as IAM roles, Kubernetes service accounts with cloud federation or OIDC from CI, which give short-lived credentials with no stored secret at all. Where a secret is needed, fetch it at runtime from a manager such as Vault, AWS Secrets Manager or Kubernetes secrets, or read it from environment variables or a mounted file with tight permissions, keeping lifetime in memory short. Use the secrets module, not random, for generating tokens. Rotate credentials regularly and support rotation by re-reading them, and scope them to least privilege. Redact secrets in logs, exceptions and tracebacks, and avoid printing request headers. Use getpass for interactive entry. Compare secrets with hmac.compare_digest to avoid timing leaks. Pre-commit hooks and scanners such as gitleaks catch accidental commits, and a leaked secret is revoked and rotated at once, not just removed from history. Dependencies and containers are checked so images contain no baked-in secrets.`,
    f: ["Why avoid secrets in command-line arguments?","What is better than a long-lived key?"]
  },
  97: {
    a: `When an orchestrator or systemd stops a process, it sends SIGTERM and later SIGKILL after a grace period, so the program should use that window to finish cleanly. I register a signal handler with the signal module, or the event loop's add_signal_handler in asyncio, that sets a shutdown flag rather than doing heavy work in the handler. The main loop checks the flag, stops accepting new work, finishes or checkpoints in-flight items, closes connections, flushes buffers and logs, and exits with a proper code. Workers consuming from a queue stop fetching, complete current messages and acknowledge them, or return unfinished ones for redelivery. Web services fail readiness first so the load balancer drains traffic, then wait for open requests. Thread and process pools are shut down with wait semantics, and timeouts cap the total so the process exits before SIGKILL, which should be configured via terminationGracePeriodSeconds or TimeoutStopSec. Work should be idempotent, since a hard kill can still happen. Testing sends SIGTERM during processing and checks no data is lost.`,
    f: ["Why set a flag instead of working in the signal handler?","What happens if the grace period expires?"]
  },
  98: {
    a: `I assume failure and design for it. Every call has connect and read timeouts. Transient errors are retried with backoff and jitter, honouring Retry-After for 429 responses, and a circuit breaker stops hammering a dependency that is down. To respect rate limits, I throttle on the client side with a token bucket or a limiter library, spread work over time and use bounded concurrency, instead of bursting and being blocked. Pagination is handled completely with cursors, and large listings use filters and incremental sync with a last-modified marker. Responses are validated against a schema, since APIs change, and unknown fields are tolerated. Results can be cached where freshness allows, using ETags and conditional requests. For writes, idempotency keys prevent duplicates on retry. I log request ids for support tickets, record metrics for latency, errors and throttling, and put API calls behind a thin client class, so behaviour is centralised and easily mocked in tests. For long jobs, checkpoints let me resume rather than restart after a failure.`,
    f: ["How do you respect a rate limit proactively?","Why wrap the API in a client class?"]
  },
  99: {
    a: `Large jobs fail partway, so the design must handle that. I process items in bounded chunks rather than loading everything into memory, using generators and itertools batching, and apply concurrency within limits. Each item is processed independently, with errors caught per item and recorded, with the item id and reason, so one bad record does not abort a million good ones, though a failure-rate threshold stops the run if something is systematically wrong. Progress is logged and exposed as metrics, such as processed, failed and remaining counts. Checkpointing stores the last completed item or batch in durable storage so a rerun resumes, and idempotent processing makes reprocessing safe. Failed items go to a dead-letter file or queue for inspection and later retry. Bulk APIs reduce call counts, and rate limits are respected. At the end, the job prints a summary with totals and exits non-zero if failures exceed tolerance. A dry-run option and a limit option help test on a small sample before a full run.`,
    f: ["Why record failed items individually?","What is a failure-rate threshold for?"]
  },
  100: {
    a: `Frequent problems include shell=True with string-built commands, which invites injection and quoting bugs; ignoring return codes and swallowing exceptions with a bare except, which hides failures; no timeouts on network calls, so scripts hang; hard-coded credentials, hosts and paths; mutable default arguments; parsing text output of tools where a library or JSON output exists; global state and everything in one huge file with no functions or tests; printing instead of logging; no dry-run for destructive actions; assuming the happy path with no pagination, so only the first page of results is processed; time handling in local time instead of UTC; installing into the system Python; and no idempotency or locking. Fixes follow the practices discussed: argument lists with check=True and timeouts, specific exceptions, configuration and secrets outside code, structured logging, a main function with argparse, type hints and tests, virtual environments with pinned dependencies, dry-run and confirmation, and code review. A linter and formatter in CI, such as ruff, catch many of these automatically.`,
    f: ["Why avoid shell=True?","What does a bare except hide?"]
  },
  101: {
    a: `The data model is the set of special methods, written with double underscores, that Python calls to implement language features. Defining them lets your classes integrate with built-in syntax. The repr and str methods control how an object prints, which is vital for useful logs and debugging; len, getitem and iter make a class sized, indexable and iterable; eq and hash control equality and use in sets and dictionaries; enter and exit make it a context manager; call makes an instance callable; and lt and related methods enable sorting. For operations tooling, a small class representing a host, a resource or a time window becomes much easier to use when it prints clearly and compares naturally. Dataclasses generate many of these for you. Care is needed with hash and equality: objects that compare equal must have equal hashes, and mutable objects used as dictionary keys cause bugs. Implementing the protocol methods, rather than inventing custom method names, makes code idiomatic and compatible with standard library functions like sorted, sum and any.`,
    f: ["Why must equal objects have equal hashes?","Which methods make an object iterable?"]
  },
  102: {
    a: `These standard modules provide efficient building blocks that replace hand-written loops. From itertools, chain joins several iterables lazily, islice takes a slice from any iterator, which suits reading the first lines of a huge file, batched or a small helper splits work into chunks for API calls, groupby groups consecutive items after sorting, product and combinations generate test matrices, and takewhile and dropwhile process streams until a condition changes. Because they are lazy, they handle data larger than memory. From functools, lru_cache memoises expensive pure functions such as repeated DNS or metadata lookups, partial pre-fills arguments to create specialised callbacks, reduce folds a sequence, wraps preserves metadata when writing decorators, and cached_property computes an attribute once per instance. Singledispatch provides function overloading by type. Using them makes code shorter and often faster, and communicates intent. The caveats are that groupby needs sorted input, lru_cache needs hashable arguments and can retain memory, and cached results must not hide data that changes over time.`,
    f: ["Why does groupby need sorted input?","What is the risk of lru_cache for changing data?"]
  },
  103: {
    a: `Variables in Python are names bound to objects, so assignment copies the reference, not the object. Two names can point to the same list, and changing it through one changes what the other sees. Passing a list or dictionary to a function lets that function modify the caller's data, which surprises people. A shallow copy, made with the copy method, slicing or the copy module, creates a new container but shares the nested objects, so modifying an inner list still affects both. A deep copy recursively duplicates everything, at a cost in time and memory, and can fail or misbehave for objects holding connections or locks. Common bugs include mutating shared default arguments, building a list of lists with multiplication, which repeats the same inner list, modifying a configuration dictionary meant to be a template, and accidentally changing data while iterating over it. Safer patterns are to treat inputs as read-only, return new objects, use tuples or frozen dataclasses for immutable data, and copy deliberately at the boundary where a function needs its own version.`,
    f: ["Why does a list of lists made with multiplication misbehave?","When is a deep copy a problem?"]
  },
  104: {
    a: `The collections module offers containers that remove a lot of boilerplate. Counter counts hashable items and provides most_common, which is ideal for top error codes, busiest clients or most frequent log messages from a stream. Defaultdict creates missing entries automatically with a factory, so grouping items by key needs no existence check, such as collecting instances by region. Deque is a double-ended queue with fast appends and pops at both ends, and a maxlen argument makes a fixed-size sliding window, useful for the last N log lines or a moving average. Namedtuple creates lightweight immutable records, though dataclasses are often clearer. ChainMap layers several dictionaries without copying, which models configuration precedence of defaults, file and environment. OrderedDict matters less since normal dictionaries preserve insertion order, though it still offers move_to_end for LRU-style structures. Choosing the right container makes code shorter and faster, because operations like pop from the front of a list are slow compared with a deque.`,
    f: ["Why use deque instead of a list for a queue?","How does ChainMap model config precedence?"]
  },
  105: {
    a: `Time bugs are common in operations code, so I follow firm rules. Store and compute in UTC, convert to a local zone only for display. Use timezone-aware datetime objects: a naive datetime has no zone and is ambiguous, and mixing naive and aware values raises errors or silently gives wrong results. The zoneinfo module in the standard library provides IANA zones, and datetime.now with a UTC zone replaces the deprecated utcnow that returns a naive value. Parse ISO 8601 strings with fromisoformat, which handles offsets, and format with isoformat. For durations use timedelta, not manual arithmetic on seconds, and for monotonic measurement of elapsed time, such as timeouts and benchmarks, use time.monotonic because the wall clock can jump with adjustments. Daylight saving changes make local-time schedules tricky, with skipped and repeated hours, so scheduling should be in UTC or handled by tools that understand zones. APIs and logs should carry timestamps with explicit offsets, and tests should freeze the clock rather than depend on the real time.`,
    f: ["Why use monotonic for timeouts?","What is wrong with a naive datetime?"]
  },
  106: {
    a: `Entry points are metadata declared in a package's configuration that other tools can discover. The console_scripts group tells the installer to generate an executable wrapper in the environment's bin directory, so a function in your package becomes a normal command such as mytool. The wrapper imports the module and calls the function, using the right interpreter and environment, which avoids path problems, shebang issues and the need to copy scripts around. Running python script.py depends on the current directory and the interpreter on the path, and the script cannot easily import its own package without path hacks. With entry points, the code is properly installed, versioned and testable, dependencies are declared, and the same command works in every environment, including pipx installs. Other groups allow plugins: a package can register an extension under a named group, and the host application loads it dynamically through importlib.metadata. In pyproject.toml, the scripts table defines console commands. This is how well-behaved tools, such as pytest plugins, are wired together.`,
    f: ["Where are entry points declared?","How do plugins use entry point groups?"]
  },
  107: {
    a: `Static type checkers find a class of bugs before runtime, but a legacy codebase cannot be fully typed overnight, so adoption is incremental. I start by adding a checker to CI with a lenient configuration that ignores missing imports and untyped functions, so it passes on day one. Then I tighten step by step: type new code fully, enable stricter options per module through configuration overrides, and annotate the most important boundaries first, such as public functions, data structures and API clients. Dataclasses, TypedDict and Protocol give structure to dictionaries and duck-typed objects. Third-party libraries without annotations use stub packages. The any type is a temporary escape and I track and reduce it. The checker runs in pre-commit and CI as a required check, with a baseline so existing errors do not block while new ones do. Benefits include safer refactors, better editor completion and living documentation. I balance value against effort, avoiding contortions for dynamic code, and use runtime validation with pydantic for external input, since types are not enforced at runtime.`,
    f: ["Why is incremental adoption better?","What does a type checker not guarantee?"]
  },
  108: {
    a: `Packages with native code need compiled artefacts. The easiest route is binary wheels: modern versions of cryptography, lxml, numpy and psycopg publish prebuilt wheels for major platforms, so pip installs them with no compiler. The problems arise on unusual platforms, such as Alpine Linux with musl, new architectures like ARM, or very new Python versions where wheels are not yet published, and then pip builds from source and needs headers and a compiler, with failures like missing libpq or openssl development files. Solutions include choosing a base image with wheel support, installing the required system packages and build tools in the image build stage and discarding them with a multi-stage build, using the binary variants of packages such as psycopg binary for convenience, and pinning versions. For production images, I prefer to build wheels once in CI, store them in an internal index and install from there, giving reproducible and fast installs. Documentation lists system requirements, and tests run on the same base image as production to catch missing libraries early.`,
    f: ["Why does Alpine cause build issues?","How does a multi-stage build help?"]
  },
  109: {
    a: `Conflicts happen when two packages require incompatible versions of a third. Pip reports the resolution failure or installs a combination with warnings. To diagnose, I use pip check to list broken requirements, pip show and pipdeptree to display who depends on what, and the resolver's verbose output to see which constraints clash. Fixes depend on the cause. I can relax an overly strict pin in my own requirements, upgrade the packages involved to versions that agree, replace a library that is unmaintained and holds back others, or isolate incompatible tools in separate environments, for example installing command-line tools with pipx so they do not share dependencies with the application. Constraints files keep transitive versions consistent across several requirement sets. Lock files from pip-tools, Poetry or uv record a tested, conflict-free set, and updates are done deliberately by a bot, with CI testing the result. Upper bounds on dependencies are avoided in libraries but used for applications through locks. Keeping the dependency tree small prevents many conflicts in the first place.`,
    f: ["Which tools show the dependency tree?","Why isolate tools with pipx?"]
  },
  110: {
    a: `Copying code leads to divergence and fixes that never reach every tool, so I prefer a shared library with a clear boundary. In a monorepo, a common package lives beside the tools and is installed in editable mode during development, so changes are visible immediately, with one set of tests and a single lint configuration. Workspaces in tools like uv or Poetry manage the relationships, and CI builds only what changed. In separate repositories, the shared library is published to an internal package index with semantic versions, and tools pin compatible ranges, giving independent release cycles at the cost of coordination and upgrade work. I keep the library small and stable, with logging, configuration, API clients and retry helpers, and avoid turning it into a dumping ground. Public interfaces are documented and tested, deprecations come with warnings and a transition period, and breaking changes need a major version. A changelog and automated dependency updates help consumers keep current. The choice depends on team size and release independence.`,
    f: ["What belongs in a shared library?","How do you handle breaking changes?"]
  },
  111: {
    a: `SQLite is a file-based database in the standard library, ideal for small tools that need structured local state without running a server. Examples are tracking which items a batch job has processed, caching API results with timestamps, storing inventory snapshots for querying and recording run history. It supports SQL, transactions and indexes, and handles concurrent readers with a single writer. Good practice is using parameterised queries to avoid injection, wrapping writes in transactions via a context manager, enabling write-ahead logging for better concurrency, setting a busy timeout so writers wait rather than fail, and keeping the database file on local disk, because network filesystems can corrupt it. Schema changes need a simple migration approach, such as a version table. A connection should not be shared across threads without care. Compared with JSON files, SQLite is safer for partial writes and crashes, and queries scale better. It is not suitable for many concurrent writers or multi-host access, where PostgreSQL or a managed store is the right choice.`,
    f: ["Why avoid SQLite on network filesystems?","What does write-ahead logging improve?"]
  },
  112: {
    a: `Writing directly to a target file risks leaving it half written if the process is killed, the disk fills or the machine loses power, and readers may see partial content. The safe pattern is to write the new content to a temporary file in the same directory, flush and fsync it so the data reaches the disk, and then rename it over the target using os.replace, which is atomic on the same filesystem. Readers see either the complete old file or the complete new one. The temporary file must be on the same filesystem as the target, otherwise the rename becomes a copy. Permissions and ownership should be set on the temporary file before the rename. For critical data, fsync the containing directory as well. The tempfile module with the delete option off and a named temporary file in the target directory makes this easy, and libraries such as atomicwrites package the pattern. For logs and databases, append-only writes or transactional stores are more appropriate than rewriting files.`,
    f: ["Why must the temp file be in the same directory?","What does os.replace guarantee?"]
  },
  113: {
    a: `Jinja2 renders text templates with variables, loops and conditions, and is a good fit for generating configuration for services like Nginx, systemd units or Prometheus rules. I load templates from a directory with a file system loader and create an environment with strict undefined behaviour, so a missing variable raises an error instead of producing an empty string and a broken file. Autoescape is off for non-HTML files but on for HTML. Whitespace control options such as trim_blocks and lstrip_blocks keep the output tidy. Data comes from validated structures, ideally typed through pydantic or dataclasses, so templates stay simple and logic remains in Python. Rendered output is validated before use, for example by running the target program's config test command, and written atomically. Custom filters add reusable formatting. A dry-run mode prints a diff against the existing file. Templates are version-controlled with the code and tested by rendering sample data and comparing to expected output. User-supplied template content should not be rendered, because template injection is a risk, so sandboxed environments are used if needed.`,
    f: ["Why use strict undefined?","How do you validate rendered configs?"]
  },
  114: {
    a: `There are two approaches: polling and event notifications. Polling periodically checks modification times or content hashes, which is simple and portable but has latency and cost, and is fine for a handful of files checked every few seconds. Event-based watching uses operating system facilities such as inotify on Linux through libraries like watchdog or watchfiles, delivering notifications when files are created, modified, moved or deleted. Events can be noisy: editors write temporary files and rename, one save may trigger several events, and events can arrive before a write finishes, so I debounce them and re-read the file after a short quiet period. For configuration reload, I parse and validate the new content first, and only swap it in if valid, keeping the old configuration and logging an error otherwise. Signals such as SIGHUP are a conventional alternative, letting an operator trigger a reload deliberately. On Kubernetes, mounted config maps update through symlink swaps, so watching the directory is more reliable than watching a single file path. Watchers must be stopped cleanly on shutdown.`,
    f: ["Why debounce file events?","Why validate before swapping config?"]
  },
  115: {
    a: `For analytics-style data, binary formats are more compact and faster than CSV and preserve types. Parquet is a columnar format that compresses well and supports efficient column selection and predicate pushdown; pandas, Polars, PyArrow and DuckDB read and write it, and it suits cost exports, inventory snapshots and log summaries. For spreadsheets, openpyxl reads and writes xlsx files with formatting, formulas and multiple sheets, and pandas wraps it for simple dataframe export, while xlsxwriter is a fast write-only alternative with charts. These are useful when the audience is non-technical and wants a file they can open. Good practice includes specifying schemas and dtypes, using compression, partitioning large datasets by date, avoiding loading everything into memory by reading column subsets or batches, and never trusting macros or formulas from untrusted spreadsheets. Excel files from users can contain surprises such as merged cells, dates stored as numbers and hidden sheets, so validation is important. Sensitive data should be protected at rest, and exports should record generation time and source.`,
    f: ["Why is Parquet better than CSV for analytics?","What problems appear in user-supplied spreadsheets?"]
  },
  116: {
    a: `The rich library makes command-line output clear without much code. It prints coloured, styled text, renders tables with aligned columns, shows progress bars with elapsed and remaining time, displays syntax-highlighted code and formatted tracebacks, and can render Markdown. For operations tools, a table of resources with status colours is far easier to scan than raw lines, and a progress bar assures users that a long job is alive. Good practice is to respect the environment: detect whether output is a terminal and disable colours and animation when it is redirected to a file or pipe, honour the NO_COLOR convention, and provide a machine-readable mode, for example JSON output, so scripts can consume results without parsing decorated text. Human output goes to standard output when it is the result, while progress and diagnostics go to standard error. Logs use the logging module, optionally with a rich handler for development. In continuous integration, plain output avoids control characters polluting logs. Keeping presentation separate from logic makes testing easier.`,
    f: ["Why offer JSON output as well?","What should happen when output is piped?"]
  },
  117: {
    a: `Interactive input should be used sparingly and never in a way that breaks automation. For secrets, the getpass module reads input without echoing it, and click and typer offer a prompt with hide_input. Secrets should not be accepted as command-line arguments, because they appear in shell history and in the process list for all users; instead accept them from environment variables, files with restricted permissions, standard input or a secrets manager. Confirmation prompts protect destructive actions, but a tool must also support non-interactive use with an explicit yes flag, and should detect when standard input is not a terminal and fail with a clear message rather than hang waiting. Prompts should offer sensible defaults and validate answers, repeating on bad input. Sensitive values are never logged, and are cleared from memory where practical, although Python offers limited guarantees. For operations tools run by humans, a typed confirmation, such as entering the environment name before a production change, reduces mistakes more than a simple yes.`,
    f: ["Why not pass secrets as arguments?","How do you support both interactive and CI use?"]
  },
  118: {
    a: `Completion and clear help make a tool pleasant and discoverable. Frameworks such as click and typer generate completion scripts for bash, zsh and fish, and argcomplete adds completion to argparse programs; users install the generated script into their shell configuration, and dynamic completions can query live data, such as environment or cluster names, though they must be fast and fail quietly. Help text is written for operators: a one-line summary for each command, descriptions for each option with defaults and allowed values, examples in the epilog, and consistent naming of flags, with long and short forms. Subcommands group related actions, and the help output shows them clearly. Error messages say what went wrong and how to fix it, and exit codes are documented. Version output includes the tool version and, optionally, dependency information for bug reports. A man page or markdown reference can be generated from the command definitions so documentation never drifts from the code. These details reduce support questions and mistakes.`,
    f: ["How does argcomplete work with argparse?","What belongs in the epilog?"]
  },
  119: {
    a: `A growing tool needs structure so it stays maintainable. Subcommands group actions under one executable, like git does, with click groups or typer apps, argparse subparsers, each command in its own module and a thin main that registers them. Shared options such as verbosity, configuration file and output format are defined once and passed through a context object holding the loaded configuration and clients, which also makes testing easier. Business logic lives in separate functions or classes with no dependency on the CLI framework, so it can be called from tests, other tools and a web interface. For extensibility, a plugin mechanism lets teams add commands without changing the core: packages register commands under an entry point group, and the core discovers them at start-up through importlib.metadata. Lazy loading keeps start-up fast by importing a command's module only when it runs. Consistent conventions for naming, flags, exit codes and output formats across all commands give a uniform experience, enforced by tests and code review.`,
    f: ["Why keep business logic out of command functions?","How do plugins register commands?"]
  },
  120: {
    a: `A tool that starts subprocesses must make sure they do not outlive it or leak resources. With subprocess, I keep a reference to each Popen object, use context managers where possible, and set timeouts on waits. When the tool receives SIGINT or SIGTERM, a handler sets a flag or raises an exception so the main flow reaches its cleanup code in finally blocks, which terminates children, first politely with terminate and then with kill after a grace period. Starting children in their own process group, with start_new_session, allows signalling the whole group so grandchildren die too. Without that, a shell wrapper may leave orphans running. Temporary files and locks are removed in finally or through context managers and atexit handlers, though a hard kill bypasses them, so stale lock detection is needed. Exit codes reflect the cause, for example 130 for interrupt, so callers can react. Avoid shell=True and pass argument lists. Testing sends signals to a running instance and verifies no processes remain.`,
    f: ["Why start children in a new process group?","What can atexit not handle?"]
  },
  121: {
    a: `Service-to-service integrations commonly use the OAuth2 client credentials flow: the client posts its id and secret to the token endpoint and receives a short-lived access token, which it sends as a bearer token on API calls. A robust client caches the token in memory together with its expiry, refreshes it shortly before it expires, instead of requesting one per call, and handles a 401 response by fetching a new token once and retrying the request. The refresh logic must be thread-safe, using a lock so concurrent workers do not all request tokens at once. Libraries such as requests-oauthlib and authlib implement the flow, and httpx supports custom auth classes that attach tokens automatically. Client secrets come from a secrets manager or environment, never source code, and are never logged. Where the platform offers workload identity, such as cloud metadata or federated tokens, that is preferable because no long-lived secret exists. Scopes are requested minimally. Tests use a fake token endpoint to cover expiry, refresh failure and revoked credentials.`,
    f: ["Why cache and pre-refresh the token?","What do you do on a 401?"]
  },
  122: {
    a: `Httpx offers a requests-like interface with both synchronous and asynchronous clients, HTTP/2 support and strict timeouts by default. For many calls, I create one AsyncClient and reuse it, since it keeps a connection pool and avoids repeated TCP and TLS handshakes. Requests are launched concurrently with asyncio gather or a task group, but bounded with a semaphore so I do not overload the server or exhaust file descriptors, and the client limits settings control the pool size. Timeouts are configured for connect, read, write and pool acquisition separately. Retries use the transport retries option for connection failures, plus backoff logic for status codes. Results are collected with exceptions captured per request, so one failure does not cancel the rest unless I want it to. The client is closed with a context manager. Compared with threads, async handles thousands of waiting I/O tasks with low overhead, but blocking libraries inside async code stall everything, so those calls go to an executor. For small jobs, a synchronous client is simpler.`,
    f: ["Why reuse one client?","Why bound concurrency with a semaphore?"]
  },
  123: {
    a: `Serverless functions suit event-driven operations tasks such as reacting to an alarm, tagging new resources, processing a queue message or running a scheduled check. The handler receives an event and context, does a bounded amount of work and returns. Good practice: keep functions small and single-purpose; create SDK clients outside the handler so warm invocations reuse connections; load configuration from environment variables and secrets from a secrets manager with caching; make the work idempotent because events can be delivered more than once; handle partial failures for batch events by reporting failed items; and set sensible timeouts, memory and concurrency limits. Dependencies are packaged in the deployment archive, a layer or a container image, with attention to size and cold start time. Structured logging with request identifiers, metrics and tracing aid debugging. Permissions follow least privilege on the function's role. Dead-letter queues capture failures for inspection. Local testing uses unit tests with mocked clients and tools like the SAM CLI, and infrastructure is defined as code. Long-running or heavy jobs belong in containers or batch services.`,
    f: ["Why create clients outside the handler?","Why must handlers be idempotent?"]
  },
  124: {
    a: `Both clouds provide libraries that implement a chain of credential sources, so code works unchanged from a laptop to production. For Azure, DefaultAzureCredential from the azure-identity package tries environment settings, workload identity, managed identity, the Azure CLI login and more, in order, so on a virtual machine or container with a managed identity no secret is needed. For Google Cloud, application default credentials are discovered from the environment, attached service accounts on compute services, workload identity on Kubernetes, or the gcloud login during development, and the client libraries pick them up automatically. In CI, OIDC federation lets the pipeline exchange its own signed token for short-lived cloud credentials, with no stored secret. Permissions are granted to the identity with least privilege roles, scoped to the resource group or project. Service account key files are avoided, and if one is unavoidable it is stored in a secrets manager, rotated and monitored. Code should accept a credential object as a parameter for testability, and logs must never include tokens.`,
    f: ["What is DefaultAzureCredential?","Why avoid service account key files?"]
  },
  125: {
    a: `Many APIs return results in pages with a cursor, a next link or page numbers. Rather than scattering loops through the code, I wrap pagination in a generator function that yields items one at a time, hiding the paging mechanics. It requests the first page, yields each item, follows the next token until none remains, and respects rate limits and retries inside the request helper. Callers simply iterate, and because it is lazy, memory use stays low and processing can start before all pages arrive; they can stop early with islice. Edge cases include empty pages in the middle, duplicate items across pages when data changes during iteration, a maximum page size, and tokens that expire. Where possible, I use stable ordering and a since-timestamp filter for incremental syncs. Tests feed the paginator a fake client returning a scripted sequence of pages, including errors, and check the yielded items and number of calls. Many SDKs already provide paginators, such as boto3, which I use in preference to writing my own.`,
    f: ["Why is a generator a good fit?","What edge cases should tests cover?"]
  },
  126: {
    a: `Structured concurrency in modern asyncio makes concurrent code easier to reason about. A TaskGroup starts several tasks inside an async context manager and waits for all of them at exit; if one fails, the others are cancelled and the errors are raised together as an exception group, so nothing is left running unnoticed. Gather also runs awaitables concurrently, and with return_exceptions it collects failures as results instead of raising, which suits independent best-effort operations, but a plain gather does not cancel siblings reliably on error. Timeouts are applied with asyncio.timeout as a context manager or wait_for, which cancels the inner work when the deadline passes and raises a timeout error. Cancellation is delivered as a CancelledError exception inside the task, at an await point; code must let it propagate, or clean up in finally blocks and re-raise, never swallow it. Shielding protects critical sections. Blocking calls inside tasks prevent cancellation from working, so they go to executors. Understanding these rules prevents leaks, hung programs and lost errors.`,
    f: ["What does a TaskGroup do on failure?","Why must CancelledError propagate?"]
  },
  127: {
    a: `For running commands on many items, I choose between threads, asyncio subprocesses and process pools depending on the workload. Because subprocesses do their work outside Python, a ThreadPoolExecutor calling subprocess.run is simple and effective: each thread waits for its child, and the GIL is not a problem. Futures return results and exceptions, and as_completed processes them as they finish. For a very large number of concurrent commands, asyncio.create_subprocess_exec avoids thread overhead and integrates with other async code, with a semaphore to limit concurrency. In both models I set a maximum number of workers appropriate to the machine and the target systems, pass arguments as lists, capture output with limits to avoid memory blow-ups, apply timeouts and kill commands that exceed them. Results are collected into a structure with the item, return code, output and duration, so failures are reported clearly at the end, and the exit code of the tool reflects whether any failed. Output from parallel commands should be tagged or buffered per item so lines are not interleaved.`,
    f: ["Why do threads work fine for subprocesses?","How do you avoid interleaved output?"]
  },
  128: {
    a: `Even with the GIL, thread-unsafe code can break because operations that look simple, such as incrementing a counter or check-then-act on a dictionary, are made of several bytecode steps and threads can interleave between them. Thread-safe designs avoid shared mutable state, or protect it. A Lock or RLock guards a critical section, used as a context manager so it is always released; keep sections short and acquire multiple locks in a consistent order to avoid deadlock. A Queue is the best way to pass work and results between threads, since it handles locking internally and supports bounded sizes for backpressure. Thread-local storage gives each thread its own copy of data, such as a database connection or an HTTP session, where sharing is unsafe. Immutable data and message passing remove most hazards. Events and conditions coordinate threads, and daemon threads are avoided for important work because they are killed abruptly at exit. Libraries are checked for thread safety, as many clients are not. Tests with stress loops and tools such as thread sanitiser equivalents help find races.`,
    f: ["Why is incrementing a counter unsafe?","When use thread-local storage?"]
  },
  129: {
    a: `The GIL has limited CPU parallelism in threads for decades. Recent Python versions introduce an optional free-threaded build that disables the GIL, letting threads run Python code truly in parallel on multiple cores, and per-interpreter GILs with a standard library interface for sub-interpreters, which run isolated interpreters in one process with less overhead than separate processes. For operations scripts that are mostly I/O-bound, nothing changes: threads and asyncio already work well. The new options matter for CPU-heavy tasks such as parsing huge logs, compression or data crunching, where today multiprocessing with its serialisation costs is needed. Caveats are important: the free-threaded build is still maturing, some C extensions are not yet compatible and may re-enable the GIL or fail, single-thread performance can be slightly lower, and code that relied on the GIL for accidental safety needs proper locking. I would test the workload and dependencies on the new build, measure the gain, and keep production on the standard build until my libraries support it. Multiprocessing remains the reliable choice today.`,
    f: ["Which workloads benefit most?","What risks come with removing the GIL?"]
  },
  130: {
    a: `A task queue decouples submitting work from executing it. A producer enqueues a job, a broker such as Redis or RabbitMQ stores it, and worker processes pick it up, run it and store the result. It fits operations needs like long-running jobs triggered from a web interface or ChatOps command, scheduled and periodic jobs, retries with backoff, fan-out of work across many workers, rate limiting and processing events from alerts. Compared with simply starting a thread or subprocess, queues give durability, so jobs survive restarts, visibility into status, retries, concurrency control and horizontal scaling. Celery is feature-rich with scheduling, workflows and many integrations but heavier to run; RQ is simple and Redis-based; Dramatiq focuses on reliability with sensible defaults. Design points: tasks must be idempotent because delivery is at least once; arguments are small and serialisable; results are stored briefly; timeouts and dead-letter handling are set; and workers have limited privileges. For small needs, a systemd timer or cron may be enough, and cloud queues with functions can replace self-run workers.`,
    f: ["Why must tasks be idempotent?","When is cron enough?"]
  },
  131: {
    a: `Async functions must run inside an event loop, so a plain test cannot await them. Plugins such as pytest-asyncio or anyio provide markers and fixtures: a test declared as async def with the appropriate marker runs inside a loop managed by the plugin, and async fixtures can set up clients or servers and tear them down. I test behaviours rather than implementation: that concurrent calls produce the right results, that timeouts cancel work, that errors propagate from task groups and that cleanup runs. External calls are replaced with async mocks, using AsyncMock from unittest.mock or fake clients, so tests are fast and deterministic. Time-dependent logic can use a controllable clock or short real timeouts, because long sleeps slow the suite; some plugins offer fake time. Common pitfalls are forgetting to await, leaking tasks past the end of a test, sharing an event loop between tests with state, and blocking calls that hide problems. Tests should assert no pending tasks remain. Running the suite with warnings enabled surfaces unawaited coroutines.`,
    f: ["What does AsyncMock provide?","How do you avoid slow sleeps in tests?"]
  },
  132: {
    a: `Tests that call real APIs are slow, flaky and may cost money or change state, but hand-written mocks can drift from reality. Two approaches help. The responses library, for requests, and respx, for httpx, register fake endpoints with specific status codes and bodies, which is ideal for error and edge cases like rate limiting, malformed payloads and timeouts. VCR.py records real interactions into cassette files the first time a test runs and replays them afterwards, giving realistic payloads without network access. Recorded data must be scrubbed: filter authorisation headers, tokens, account identifiers and personal information before committing cassettes. Matching rules decide how requests are compared, such as method, URI and body. Cassettes are refreshed periodically, perhaps in a scheduled job against a sandbox account, to detect API changes. The test suite runs in a mode that forbids new recordings in CI, so unexpected calls fail. Contract tests against a sandbox complement them, because replayed data cannot prove the real service still behaves the same.`,
    f: ["Why scrub cassettes?","What do responses and respx offer that VCR does not?"]
  },
  133: {
    a: `Snapshot testing stores the expected output of a function in a file, and later runs compare current output with it. If it differs, the test fails and a developer reviews the change, accepting it by updating the snapshot when it is intended. It is valuable where outputs are large and structured, such as rendered configuration templates, generated Kubernetes manifests, report text, CLI help output and API responses after transformation. Instead of writing dozens of assertions, one comparison covers the whole result, and the diff in review shows exactly how behaviour changed. Libraries such as syrupy, pytest-snapshot or approvaltests manage the files. The risks are blindly accepting changes, huge snapshots nobody reads, and brittleness from volatile fields like timestamps, random identifiers and ordering, which should be normalised or sorted before comparison. Snapshots complement, not replace, targeted assertions of key properties. I keep them small, committed to the repository, reviewed in pull requests, and generated from deterministic inputs, so a change in output is always the result of a deliberate change in code.`,
    f: ["What makes snapshots brittle?","How should changes be reviewed?"]
  },
  134: {
    a: `Coverage only shows which lines ran during tests, not whether the tests would notice if the code were wrong. A test that calls a function without asserting anything still counts as coverage. Mutation testing measures test strength: a tool such as mutmut or cosmic-ray makes small deliberate changes to the code, called mutants, such as flipping a comparison, changing a constant or removing a statement, and re-runs the tests for each. If the tests still pass, the mutant survived, meaning the suite does not detect that kind of fault. The mutation score, the proportion of mutants killed, is a better indicator of effectiveness than line coverage. The costs are long run times, since the suite runs many times, and equivalent mutants that do not change behaviour. So I run it selectively on critical modules, on changed files in a scheduled job, and use the surviving mutants as a to-do list for missing assertions rather than chasing a perfect score. It helps most for logic-heavy code such as parsers, retry policies and calculation functions.`,
    f: ["What is a surviving mutant?","Why is it expensive?"]
  },
  135: {
    a: `Performance claims need measurement. For micro-benchmarks, the timeit module repeats a small snippet and reports the best time, while pytest-benchmark integrates benchmarks into the test suite, runs each many times, reports statistics such as mean, minimum and standard deviation, and can save results and compare runs, failing a build when a function becomes slower than a threshold. For larger programs, cProfile and tools like py-spy find the hot spots first, because optimising the wrong part wastes effort. Benchmarks use realistic data sizes, run on stable hardware, warm up caches and avoid noise from other processes; shared CI runners are noisy, so tolerances are generous or dedicated runners are used. Tracking results over time in a dashboard reveals gradual degradation. Memory benchmarks use tracemalloc or memray. I optimise algorithms and data structures before micro-tuning, and only after a measurable need, keeping readability. A regression test with a loose bound on an important code path, such as parsing a standard sample file, catches accidental quadratic behaviour.`,
    f: ["Why profile before optimising?","How do you handle noisy CI runners?"]
  },
  136: {
    a: `Lifecycle automation touches many systems, such as the identity provider, cloud accounts, source control, chat and ticketing, so reliability and auditability matter more than speed. The trigger comes from an authoritative source, usually the HR system or the identity provider, delivered by webhook or polled. The workflow is a list of idempotent steps per system: disable the identity provider account, revoke sessions and tokens, remove access keys and group memberships, transfer ownership of resources and shared documents, reassign on-call schedules and open tickets, and archive mailboxes according to policy. Each step is retried safely, records its outcome and continues despite failures in unrelated systems, and failures raise a ticket for manual follow-up. Destructive actions such as deleting data are delayed, with a retention window and approval. A dry-run mode and a report of what would change allow review. API credentials for the automation are narrowly scoped, stored in a secrets manager, and every action is logged with who or what triggered it. Joiners and movers use the same framework, driven by role definitions rather than copied permissions. Regular reconciliation finds accounts that escaped the workflow.`,
    f: ["Why delay destructive steps?","How do you detect accounts that were missed?"]
  },
  137: {
    a: `During incidents, engineers waste time running the same commands on different hosts. A collector script automates evidence gathering consistently and safely. It runs on a host or against a list of hosts and collects a defined set of read-only data: system load, memory and disk usage, process lists, network connections and listening ports, recent kernel and service logs, container and orchestrator state, configuration files with secrets redacted, and application-specific health output. Each command runs with a timeout and its output is captured with the timestamp and exit code, so failures are visible and a hung command cannot block the rest. The results are packaged into a compressed archive named with the host and time, written with restrictive permissions, and uploaded to a controlled location, with a size limit to avoid filling the disk. Sensitive data is filtered by patterns, and the collection is logged. It must have minimal impact, avoiding heavy commands on struggling systems, and be safe to run repeatedly. Kubernetes versions gather pod descriptions, events and logs. A runbook links to it, and the bundle attaches to the incident ticket for later analysis.`,
    f: ["Why use per-command timeouts?","How do you avoid collecting secrets?"]
  },
  138: {
    a: `Cost visibility starts with the billing export: AWS Cost and Usage Reports delivered as Parquet to object storage, Azure cost exports, or the Google billing export in BigQuery. A Python job loads the data with pandas, DuckDB or the query client, normalises it, and groups by tags such as team, environment and application, plus service, region and usage type. Reports show trends over time, month-on-month changes, the top spenders and anomalies, such as a sudden daily jump. Untagged spend is flagged with owners to chase. Finding savings combines billing data with utilisation metrics: instances with low CPU for weeks, unattached volumes, old snapshots, idle load balancers, oversized databases, missing savings plans or reserved capacity coverage, and data transfer hot spots. Recommendations include estimated monthly savings and an owner. Output goes to dashboards, spreadsheets or chat summaries, with weekly scheduling. The job uses read-only roles, and cost allocation tags must be activated for data to appear. Showback to teams drives behaviour, and automation can tag resources or stop non-production resources outside working hours.`,
    f: ["Why do tags matter for cost reports?","How do you detect cost anomalies?"]
  },
  139: {
    a: `The standard ipaddress module represents addresses and networks as objects with correct arithmetic, replacing fragile string manipulation. IPv4Network and IPv6Network parse CIDR blocks, expose the netmask, number of addresses and host iterators, test membership with the in operator, and check whether networks overlap, are subnets of one another or are private. The subnets method splits a block into smaller ones of a given prefix length, and supernet or collapse_addresses merge ranges. For planning, a script can read existing allocations from a file or IPAM system, find overlaps between VPC, on-premises and partner ranges that would block peering, calculate free blocks, and propose the next available subnet of a requested size. Validation in CI can reject proposals that overlap or fall outside approved ranges. The module handles network and broadcast addresses and edge cases such as point-to-point links. Cloud reserved addresses per subnet are accounted for separately. Output is a clear table, and the single source of truth should be an IPAM tool or version-controlled data, with the script enforcing consistency before Terraform applies changes.`,
    f: ["How do you detect overlapping ranges?","What does collapse_addresses do?"]
  },
  140: {
    a: `Capacity problems are cheaper to prevent than to fix at 3 a.m., so I forecast from history. The script pulls a series from the metrics backend, for example daily peak disk usage, memory or request rate for the last several months, through the Prometheus HTTP API with range queries. It cleans the data, handles gaps, and aggregates to a sensible resolution. For steadily growing resources, a linear regression with numpy or a library such as statsmodels projects when usage will cross a threshold, producing a days-until-full estimate. For seasonal workloads, I use models that capture weekly patterns, or compare year-on-year, and always show the uncertainty. Prometheus itself offers predict_linear for alerting. The output is a ranked report of resources predicted to hit limits within a horizon, with owners and growth rates, feeding tickets and planning. I validate forecasts against what actually happened, keep models simple and explainable, and avoid false precision, because step changes from launches or migrations break trends. Alerts for time-to-full complement the report.`,
    f: ["What does predict_linear do?","Why keep forecasting models simple?"]
  },
  141: {
    a: `Dashboards edited by hand in the interface drift, vanish and differ between environments, so I treat them as code. The Grafana HTTP API lets a script create, update and export dashboards, folders, data sources and alert rules. Dashboards are described in JSON or generated with a library such as grafanalib or the Grafana Foundation SDK in Python, which produces consistent panels from small functions, so a standard service dashboard can be created for every application from a template with the service name as a parameter. The definitions are stored in Git, reviewed in pull requests and deployed by a pipeline with an API token of limited scope, or provisioned from files on disk or through a Kubernetes sidecar. Terraform's Grafana provider is another option. Variables, consistent naming and tags help navigation. Alert rules are defined alongside dashboards with clear thresholds and runbook links. Exported JSON should be cleaned of volatile fields like ids and versions to give small diffs. Changes can be validated by loading them in a staging instance, and drift detection compares live dashboards with Git.`,
    f: ["Why generate dashboards from code?","How do you keep diffs small?"]
  },
  142: {
    a: `Applications should normally write logs to standard output or files and let an agent such as Fluent Bit, Vector or Promtail collect and ship them, because that decouples the application from the logging backend, handles buffering and retries, and keeps working when the backend is down. Direct shipping from Python is justified for scripts and jobs with no agent, serverless functions and structured audit events. Clients exist: the opensearch-py and elasticsearch libraries provide bulk helpers that send many documents in one request, and Loki accepts pushes over HTTP, with libraries or logging handlers available. Good practice is batching, using the bulk API, with timeouts, retries and backoff; running the shipping in a background thread or queue handler so application latency is unaffected; bounding memory with a queue limit and a drop policy so a slow backend cannot take down the application; and using structured JSON with consistent field names and index templates. Credentials are scoped and rotated. Logging failures must not crash the program, and a local fallback file keeps data when the backend is unreachable. Avoid sending sensitive data.`,
    f: ["Why prefer an agent for services?","How do you avoid blocking the application?"]
  },
  143: {
    a: `Internal metrics can look healthy while users cannot log in, so synthetic probes exercise the service from the outside like a user. A probe is a script that performs a realistic request sequence, such as loading a page, logging in with a test account, searching, adding an item and checking out, and verifies content, status codes and timing at each step. Libraries range from requests for API journeys to Playwright for real browser flows including JavaScript. Probes run on a schedule from multiple locations, including outside the cluster and different regions, to catch DNS, certificate, CDN and routing issues. Results become metrics, success and duration per step, scraped by Prometheus or pushed to a monitoring service, with alerts on consecutive failures and slow responses, and failure screenshots or response bodies stored for diagnosis. Test accounts and data must be isolated, safe and clearly labelled so that probes do not pollute analytics or trigger fraud rules, and secrets are managed properly. Probes should be stable to avoid false alarms, with retries only for network flukes, and their own health is monitored.`,
    f: ["Why run probes from outside the cluster?","How do you avoid flaky probes?"]
  },
  144: {
    a: `Simple methods catch most operational anomalies and are easier to explain and trust. A rolling mean and standard deviation give a z-score, flagging points several deviations away from recent behaviour. Median and median absolute deviation resist outliers better. Comparing with the same time last week handles daily and weekly seasonality, and a percentage-change threshold detects sudden jumps. Exponential smoothing or a seasonal decomposition separates trend and cycles from the residual. For rates, comparing against a forecast band works well. Implementation uses pandas or numpy on data from the Prometheus API, and Prometheus can do some of this in queries with functions such as avg_over_time, stddev_over_time and offset. Alerting on anomalies needs care: require sustained deviation, set minimum absolute thresholds to avoid noise on tiny numbers, and tune with historical data to measure false positives and misses. Machine-learning approaches are considered when seasonality is complex or there are thousands of series, but they add maintenance and opacity. Whichever method, alerts must be actionable and link to a dashboard and runbook.`,
    f: ["Why compare to the same time last week?","Why add minimum thresholds?"]
  },
  145: {
    a: `Profiling starts from a question: is the program slow because of CPU, I/O waiting or memory pressure? Cprofile, built in, records function call counts and cumulative times; sorting by cumulative or total time shows which functions dominate, and the output can be viewed with visualisation tools like snakeviz. Its overhead is moderate and it does not see inside C extensions. Sampling profilers such as py-spy attach to running processes with little overhead, ideal in production, and produce flame graphs where wide frames show where time goes. Scalene separates Python time from native time and I/O wait, and shows line-level CPU and memory use, even for GPU. For memory, tracemalloc compares snapshots to find allocation sites, and memray records every allocation with flame graphs and can find leaks and peak usage. Interpretation focuses on the biggest contributors: a hot loop, repeated parsing, an accidental quadratic algorithm, large intermediate lists or an unbounded cache. After changing code, I re-profile to confirm the gain. Profiles should use realistic inputs, because toy data hides the real hot spots.`,
    f: ["Why use a sampling profiler in production?","What does memray show?"]
  },
  146: {
    a: `Scripts that create clients inside functions and call them directly are hard to test without mocking globals. A better structure passes dependencies in: functions or classes receive the client, configuration, clock and logger as arguments, so tests supply fakes. I wrap the parts of an SDK I actually use in a small interface, for example a class with methods to list volumes and delete a volume, hiding pagination and error mapping. Business logic, such as deciding which volumes are old and unattached, works on plain data and is a pure function that is trivial to test with tables of cases. The real wrapper is tested in a few integration tests, and everything else uses an in-memory fake implementing the same interface, which is often more reliable than patching with mocks. This also makes changing providers or API versions easier. Protocols or abstract base classes document the contract. Composition happens at the edge, in main, where real clients are created. The result is smaller functions, faster tests and clearer separation between decisions and side effects.`,
    f: ["Why separate decisions from side effects?","Why prefer fakes over heavy mocking?"]
  },
  147: {
    a: `A multi-step operation like provisioning a cluster or migrating data can fail anywhere, so it should be resumable. I model it as a state machine: a defined set of states, such as requested, network created, nodes ready and verified, with allowed transitions, and each step as a function that moves the workflow to the next state. The current state and relevant data are persisted after every step in a database or a state file, so on restart the program reads the last state and continues from there. Steps are idempotent, since a crash after performing an action but before saving the state means the step runs again. Failures record the error and either retry with backoff or move to a failed state that waits for human action, with compensating steps for rollback where needed. Timeouts and a maximum number of attempts prevent endless loops. The state history provides an audit trail and visibility, and a command can show the status of all workflows. Libraries like transitions help, and workflow engines such as Temporal, Airflow or Step Functions provide this durably for complex cases.`,
    f: ["Why must steps be idempotent?","When do you adopt a workflow engine?"]
  },
  148: {
    a: `When a tool needs many similar components, such as health checks, inventory collectors or report generators, a registry pattern avoids long if-else chains and lets teams add components without editing core code. A base class or protocol defines the interface, for example a check with a name and a run method returning a result. A registry maps names to implementations, filled by a decorator that registers each class or function on import, or by discovering plugins through entry points, so installed packages can contribute. The core iterates over the registry, applying configuration to select which components run, with per-component options and error isolation, so one failing plugin is reported but does not stop the others. Each component has a timeout, and results use a common structure with status, message and metrics. Configuration validation ensures unknown names are rejected early. Tests cover each component in isolation plus the runner. Documentation explains how to write a new one in a few lines. This design keeps the tool extensible, makes behaviour uniform and encourages reuse.`,
    f: ["How does a decorator register components?","Why isolate plugin errors?"]
  },
  149: {
    a: `Rate limiting protects both your own service and the APIs you call. In a token bucket, a bucket holds up to a maximum number of tokens that refill at a steady rate; each request consumes a token, and when the bucket is empty the request waits or is rejected. This allows bursts up to the bucket size while enforcing an average rate. A sliding window counts requests in the last interval and rejects when the count exceeds the limit, which is more precise about boundaries but stores more data. For a client, I implement the bucket with a monotonic clock, a lock for thread safety, and a method that either sleeps until a token is available or reports the wait time; async code uses an asyncio lock and sleep. Libraries such as limiter packages or aiolimiter provide this. Honouring the server's rate-limit headers and Retry-After is better than guessing, and I combine limiting with retries and backoff. For limits shared across many processes or hosts, state lives in Redis, using atomic scripts. Metrics on waits and rejections show when limits bind.`,
    f: ["How does a token bucket allow bursts?","How do you share a limit across processes?"]
  },
  150: {
    a: `Some behaviour needs to change while the service runs: log level, thresholds, allow lists or the enabling of a new code path. For configuration, the service loads settings into an immutable object and replaces the whole object atomically on reload, triggered by a SIGHUP, a file change or a periodic poll of a config store, so no request sees a half-updated state. New configuration is parsed and validated first, and if invalid the old one stays active and an error is logged and counted. Code reads the current configuration through an accessor, not by caching values at import time. Feature flags add a layer: a flag service or file defines flags, possibly with percentage rollouts or targeting by user or environment, and code checks a flag at the decision point. Flags have owners and expiry dates and are removed after rollout, because stale flags create untested combinations. Defaults are safe if the flag service is unreachable. Changes are audited, and critical settings avoid dynamic changes when a restart is safer. Tests cover both flag states and reload failure.`,
    f: ["Why swap the config object atomically?","What is the risk of old flags?"]
  }
};
